WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.460 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.428 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.428 --> 00:00:07.380 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:16.730 align:middle line:90%


00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:18.300 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: OK, so
let me get started.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.380 align:middle line:84%
So if you remember
last class, I did

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.200 align:middle line:84%
the basic strategic
investment theory

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:29.780 align:middle line:84%
and talked about how we
think about firms trying

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:34.580 align:middle line:84%
to make long term investments
that either deter rivals

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:38.403 align:middle line:84%
from entering or changing
the nature of competition

00:00:38.403 --> 00:00:40.820 align:middle line:84%
in a way that's going to make
them more profitable if they

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:42.920 align:middle line:84%
go ahead and give up and
accommodate that the rivals come

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:43.560 align:middle line:90%
in.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:47.450 align:middle line:84%
And the basic insights
there are, to deter entry,

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:48.270 align:middle line:90%
it's fairly simple.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:49.760 align:middle line:84%
You just want to do whatever
kinds of investments

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.300 align:middle line:90%
are going to hurt your rivals.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:54.260 align:middle line:84%
And then when you're talking
about accommodating entry,

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:56.258 align:middle line:84%
one of the insights was
it really depends on

00:00:56.258 --> 00:00:58.550 align:middle line:84%
whether you're playing games
with strategic complements

00:00:58.550 --> 00:00:59.520 align:middle line:90%
or substitutes.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:01.400 align:middle line:84%
And in strategic
complements games,

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:05.060 align:middle line:84%
generally investments
that help your rivals

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:06.830 align:middle line:84%
are ones that are
good strategically,

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:09.300 align:middle line:84%
and investments that
hurt your rivals are bad.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.980 align:middle line:84%
And then it's reversed if you're
looking at Cournot wide games.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:16.980 align:middle line:84%
So today, I'm going to
talk about empirical work

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.430 align:middle line:90%
on strategic investment.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:21.120 align:middle line:84%
And why are we
interested in this?

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.060 align:middle line:84%
There's the standard
considerations

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.390 align:middle line:90%
that we want to understand.

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:26.470 align:middle line:90%
Do the models work well?

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:28.110 align:middle line:90%
Do firms do what we think?

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:32.340 align:middle line:84%
There are other cases
where strategic investment

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:33.990 align:middle line:84%
of the type described
in those models

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:35.790 align:middle line:84%
could be violating
antitrust laws.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:38.940 align:middle line:84%
I gave a model of
tying where tying

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.650 align:middle line:90%
could be used to deter entry.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:43.616 align:middle line:84%
That would potentially be
a monopolization violation.

00:01:43.616 --> 00:01:48.640 align:middle line:90%


00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.620 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, so yeah,
trying to see, do these--

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:54.630 align:middle line:84%
and are the incentives
that these models

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:57.132 align:middle line:84%
say could be there to signal
costs or do other things?

00:01:57.132 --> 00:01:58.840 align:middle line:84%
Are those actually
empirically important?

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:02.040 align:middle line:84%
And then if they are important,
do the firms recognize and react

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.070 align:middle line:90%
to them?

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:07.270 align:middle line:84%
So if we're thinking about, one,
are these incentives important,

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:10.170 align:middle line:84%
the main thing to
understand is this d a1 star

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:14.010 align:middle line:90%
/ dk1 and da2 star / dk1.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:17.520 align:middle line:84%
If you remember, when we're
talking about the entry

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:22.790 align:middle line:84%
deterrence, whether you
do more or less investment

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:25.370 align:middle line:84%
to deter entry was determined
by a strategic effect.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.515 align:middle line:84%
And that strategic
effect looked like--

00:02:27.515 --> 00:02:30.500 align:middle line:90%


00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.673 align:middle line:90%
it was d pi 1--

00:02:32.673 --> 00:02:36.540 align:middle line:90%


00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:42.945 align:middle line:84%
sorry, d pi 2 /
da1, da1 star / dk1.

00:02:42.945 --> 00:02:46.050 align:middle line:90%


00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:47.730 align:middle line:84%
Strategic entry
deterrence was about

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:53.590 align:middle line:84%
k1 is a commitment that makes me
change my second period action.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.800 align:middle line:84%
And then when I change
my second period action,

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:00.540 align:middle line:84%
how does it affect firm 2 and
keep firm 2 out of the market?

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:05.100 align:middle line:84%
Accommodation, I went to
the helping and hurting.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.290 align:middle line:84%
But before that, I had
the strategic effect

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:14.460 align:middle line:84%
was d pi 1 / da2,
da2 star / dk1.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:17.310 align:middle line:84%
So strategic entry
accommodation was

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:21.000 align:middle line:84%
about getting your rival to
do something that-- using k1

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.200 align:middle line:84%
as a change in the
nature of competition

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.033 align:middle line:84%
in a way that get your
rival to do something

00:03:25.033 --> 00:03:26.400 align:middle line:90%
that you want to do.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.830 align:middle line:84%
We pretty much always know
what these are-- the signs

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:30.430 align:middle line:90%
of these, d pi 2 / da1.

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:32.940 align:middle line:84%
This is just like firms
like their opponents

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.610 align:middle line:84%
to set higher prices, or
firms like their opponents

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.230 align:middle line:90%
choose lower quantities.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:38.980 align:middle line:84%
So then, the important
strategic questions

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.780 align:middle line:84%
are, how big are
these things that--

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:45.520 align:middle line:84%
how much do the a's change
with the investments?

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:48.543 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, that's
one thing people do.

00:03:48.543 --> 00:03:49.960 align:middle line:84%
And then the other
thing people do

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:57.060 align:middle line:84%
is we're trying to see, does the
model with these investment--

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.290 align:middle line:84%
entry deterring
investments better explain

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:01.800 align:middle line:84%
what firms do than a model
without those investment

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:07.230 align:middle line:84%
firms in there, and are
firms recognizing those?

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:10.470 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, we can
think of this question

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.470 align:middle line:84%
of are firms recognizing and
reacting to entry deterrence

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:15.510 align:middle line:84%
incentives by really
thinking of it as just

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:17.579 align:middle line:90%
different strategic models.

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:19.829 align:middle line:84%
So as I said, everything I
did on strategic investment

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:23.160 align:middle line:84%
was comparing the investment
with an entry accommodation

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:25.990 align:middle line:84%
motive to the open
loop equilibrium.

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:29.190 align:middle line:84%
And the open loop equilibrium
is the fully optimal choice

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:33.480 align:middle line:84%
of k in this model, where
K is never observed.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:37.690 align:middle line:84%
The incumbent chooses Kk The
entrant chooses in or out.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:41.280 align:middle line:84%
There's monopoly or duopoly,
and only after the--

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.120 align:middle line:84%
and never does the
rival observe k.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.610 align:middle line:84%
So you can think of
non-strategic investment

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:50.370 align:middle line:84%
as just strategic
investment in that model

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:51.820 align:middle line:90%
where k is unobserved.

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:55.220 align:middle line:84%
And then you could add in two
different strategic variants,

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:59.830 align:middle line:84%
one where if you
observed k here,

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:01.690 align:middle line:84%
that's the fully
strategic model where

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:03.640 align:middle line:84%
you think about entry
accommodation and entry

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:04.880 align:middle line:90%
deterrence motives.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.120 align:middle line:84%
And if you observe
k here, that's

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:09.850 align:middle line:84%
the model where you only
think about strategic entry

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:12.430 align:middle line:84%
accommodation, not deterrence,
because you're not observing k

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:13.845 align:middle line:90%
until after the entry decision.

00:05:13.845 --> 00:05:16.950 align:middle line:90%


00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:19.080 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so I'm going to
talk about papers taking

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:20.750 align:middle line:90%
a few different approaches.

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:24.480 align:middle line:84%
First approach is just examine
the effects of the investment

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:25.120 align:middle line:90%
directly.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.600 align:middle line:90%
Just look at some investment, k.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.880 align:middle line:84%
Look at some action,
a, that occurs later,

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:34.050 align:middle line:84%
and observe whether da / dk is
large, and what the sign of it

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:35.070 align:middle line:90%
is.

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:39.570 align:middle line:84%
Second approach you see is to
try to understand if firms are

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:41.940 align:middle line:84%
following their strategic
incentives, what we could do

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.460 align:middle line:84%
is examine reduced
form predictions that

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:46.860 align:middle line:84%
differ between the non-strategic
and strategic models,

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:49.680 align:middle line:84%
and then see which one
better explains the data.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:53.280 align:middle line:84%
And then the third approach
is to write down a model

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:55.977 align:middle line:84%
that you can structurally
estimate and understand,

00:05:55.977 --> 00:05:58.560 align:middle line:84%
does a structural model explain
things better than, hopefully,

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:01.890 align:middle line:84%
some alternate structural model
that doesn't have that term?

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:04.380 align:middle line:84%
And so I'll talk about
papers with each approach.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:08.460 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to start with
Chevalier's paper, Capital

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:10.950 align:middle line:84%
Structure and Product
Market Competition.

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:13.440 align:middle line:84%
So this is a paper about
the supermarket industry,

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:19.200 align:middle line:84%
and it's motivated by
work in corporate finance.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.870 align:middle line:84%
So the corporate
finance question

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:24.840 align:middle line:84%
that motivates this is, why
do firms choose the capital

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:26.700 align:middle line:90%
structures that they do?

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:27.540 align:middle line:90%
I think we're used--

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:28.332 align:middle line:90%
well, I don't know.

00:06:28.332 --> 00:06:30.790 align:middle line:84%
How many people here have taken
a corporate finance course?

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:32.160 align:middle line:90%
Is the answer going to be 0?

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:34.620 align:middle line:90%
OK, so-- one.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:35.220 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:39.030 align:middle line:84%
OK, anyway, so starting
point for most theoretical

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:43.330 align:middle line:84%
corporate finance courses is you
start with Miller- Modigliani.

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:45.330 align:middle line:84%
A lot of corporate finance
is about how does a--

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:48.090 align:middle line:84%
well, the basic structure that
you certainly want to teach MBAs

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:53.310 align:middle line:84%
is, how do you set up your
capital structure of your firm?

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.800 align:middle line:84%
How do you decide between
raising money through debt,

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:57.510 align:middle line:90%
raising money through equity?

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:01.830 align:middle line:84%
How is a firm value affected by
choosing a capital structure?

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:04.020 align:middle line:84%
And so the starting
point for this literature

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:06.610 align:middle line:84%
is what's called the
Miller-Modigliani theorem.

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:08.070 align:middle line:84%
And the
Miller-Modigliani theorem

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:09.840 align:middle line:84%
is a basic theorem
talking about how

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.370 align:middle line:84%
in competitive markets with
different states of the world,

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:17.008 align:middle line:84%
your capital structure is
just completely irrelevant.

00:07:17.008 --> 00:07:18.550 align:middle line:84%
And if you think
about it, it's like,

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:21.010 align:middle line:84%
there's just some
giant set of states

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:23.440 align:middle line:84%
of the world that may
arise, and the firm

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:26.060 align:middle line:84%
earns some different amount
of profits in every state.

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:29.320 align:middle line:84%
And then you divide up the
state contingent claims

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:32.350 align:middle line:84%
on the firms into
a debt contract,

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:35.480 align:middle line:84%
or an equity contract-- like
any contract, like equity,

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:37.480 align:middle line:84%
or debt, or whatever,
pays off different amounts

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:39.110 align:middle line:84%
in different states
of the world.

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:41.770 align:middle line:84%
And then Miller-Modigliani
theorem is basically saying,

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:43.520 align:middle line:84%
look, if you think about it, if
you're just deciding how much

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:44.620 align:middle line:84%
to have in different
states of the world,

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:46.995 align:middle line:84%
whether you have some this
combination of debt and equity

00:07:46.995 --> 00:07:50.205 align:middle line:84%
or this combination, all you're
doing is changing the debt

00:07:50.205 --> 00:07:52.330 align:middle line:84%
contract or something, the
equities were something,

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:54.700 align:middle line:90%
but the sum of the two is--

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:57.070 align:middle line:84%
or the sum of all the
claims you issue on the firm

00:07:57.070 --> 00:07:59.643 align:middle line:84%
are still just issued to all
of the value of the state

00:07:59.643 --> 00:08:01.310 align:middle line:84%
contingent profits
you're going to earn.

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:04.990 align:middle line:84%
So capital structure is
just completely irrelevant.

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:07.180 align:middle line:84%
So then obviously, after
that first observation,

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:10.570 align:middle line:84%
you need theories
for why firms do

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:13.370 align:middle line:84%
have debt, and do have equity,
and why they decide to do it.

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:16.060 align:middle line:84%
And I wouldn't say this
is the biggest class,

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:18.640 align:middle line:84%
but a substantial part of
that literature does say,

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:21.610 align:middle line:84%
one reason why you could want
capital structure could matter

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:25.060 align:middle line:84%
is because capital structure
can affect the product market

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:26.780 align:middle line:90%
competition between the firms.

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:29.200 align:middle line:84%
And there's a big
literature about debt,

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:33.460 align:middle line:84%
arguing how debt could affect
real actions that firms take,

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:36.490 align:middle line:84%
and effects that can go
in different directions.

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:40.059 align:middle line:84%
So let's think about, thought
experiment, a firm takes

00:08:40.059 --> 00:08:41.200 align:middle line:90%
on a large amount of debt.

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:43.600 align:middle line:84%
And so because they've taken
on a large amount of debt,

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:46.210 align:middle line:84%
they need a lot of cash flow
to make the debt payments

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:48.610 align:middle line:90%
that they've taken on.

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:49.940 align:middle line:90%
What does that do?

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:53.860 align:middle line:84%
Well, first, start with a
standard oligopoly game.

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:55.610 align:middle line:84%
That's going to have
no effect whatsoever.

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:57.500 align:middle line:90%
So I take it on debt.

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:58.850 align:middle line:90%
I want to make more money.

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:00.820 align:middle line:84%
If it's a static
game, every period

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:02.590 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to make as
much money as I can.

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:04.882 align:middle line:84%
So I'm still just going to
choose the profit maximizing

00:09:04.882 --> 00:09:06.370 align:middle line:90%
prices in every period.

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:09.990 align:middle line:84%
Debt would have no
effect whatsoever.

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:11.740 align:middle line:84%
To get debt to have
an effect, though, you

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:14.630 align:middle line:90%
can think about dynamic models.

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:19.410 align:middle line:84%
So one class of model might
be if you have a model where

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:20.610 align:middle line:90%
you have customer loyalty.

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:22.660 align:middle line:84%
You sell things to
consumers at good prices.

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:23.920 align:middle line:90%
They get used to your product.

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:25.545 align:middle line:84%
They like the taste
of your mayonnaise.

00:09:25.545 --> 00:09:28.440 align:middle line:84%
They like your
frequent flyer miles.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:31.210 align:middle line:84%
And then you build up
this goodwill over time,

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:33.990 align:middle line:84%
but then goodwill is an asset
that you can depreciate.

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:37.110 align:middle line:84%
Well, if you now need a lot
of money in the short run

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:39.720 align:middle line:84%
to pay down your debt, you
might start raising your prices,

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:42.990 align:middle line:84%
exploit all the customers who
are temporarily captive to you,

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:46.650 align:middle line:84%
and exploit and burn up all
the goodwill you've gotten.

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:49.320 align:middle line:84%
So that might be a case
where you raise prices

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:52.890 align:middle line:84%
because you need to raise
money in the short run.

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:55.420 align:middle line:84%
In dynamic collusion, you
can get the opposite effect.

00:09:55.420 --> 00:09:57.360 align:middle line:84%
I talked about in the
Rotemberg Saloner model,

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:00.630 align:middle line:84%
if firms have a big incentive
to deviate from the cartel

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:02.550 align:middle line:84%
agreement, then
the full collusion

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:04.420 align:middle line:90%
is no longer sustainable.

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:06.870 align:middle line:84%
So in a dynamic
model, you could have

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:10.200 align:middle line:84%
that we can no longer sustain
this high collusive prices

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:11.585 align:middle line:90%
because we have the debt.

00:10:11.585 --> 00:10:13.710 align:middle line:84%
We need to lower our prices
to reduce our incentive

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:15.670 align:middle line:90%
DV on the cartel agreement.

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:21.010 align:middle line:84%
So this need for short run money
could raise or lower prices.

00:10:21.010 --> 00:10:25.300 align:middle line:84%
Another effect we talked
about with debt is high debt--

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:28.390 align:middle line:90%
like if there's a--

00:10:28.390 --> 00:10:29.950 align:middle line:84%
for every extra
dollar you borrow,

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:32.283 align:middle line:84%
lenders are going to demand
a higher and higher interest

00:10:32.283 --> 00:10:35.110 align:middle line:84%
rate for the next dollar
because the threat of bankruptcy

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:36.730 align:middle line:90%
is larger.

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:39.910 align:middle line:84%
So high debt raises
the cost of capital

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:42.160 align:middle line:84%
and makes it difficult
for you to borrow more

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:44.000 align:middle line:90%
money for future expansion.

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:46.330 align:middle line:84%
And so if you think
about, how would

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:49.510 align:middle line:84%
this work in a standard
Cournot competition model,

00:10:49.510 --> 00:10:53.770 align:middle line:84%
in a Cournot model, if you've
committed to not expand,

00:10:53.770 --> 00:10:55.880 align:middle line:84%
then your rivals
are going to expand.

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:58.060 align:middle line:84%
Because if I commit
to a low Q, my rivals

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:00.620 align:middle line:84%
are going to choose a
higher Q as a best response.

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:04.600 align:middle line:84%
So it could be that debt
is a commitment that

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:07.270 align:middle line:90%
leads my rivals to expand more.

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:08.870 align:middle line:84%
There could be other
models, though.

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:09.880 align:middle line:90%
I haven't talked about them yet.

00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:11.710 align:middle line:84%
Like if you have--
there's some new market,

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:14.380 align:middle line:90%
there's some new growing city.

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:15.940 align:middle line:84%
One of two firms
is going to open up

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:17.960 align:middle line:84%
a store in that
new growing city.

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:19.030 align:middle line:90%
Well, if you know--

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:21.338 align:middle line:84%
like if you are afraid
your rival is going

00:11:21.338 --> 00:11:23.380 align:middle line:84%
to open before you, you
may race and get in there

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:24.980 align:middle line:84%
before it's short
run profitable,

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:28.040 align:middle line:84%
but just when the PDV
is positive, to get in

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:29.810 align:middle line:84%
and beat your rival
into the new market.

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:33.530 align:middle line:84%
If you know your rival is debt
constrained, can't raise money,

00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:36.390 align:middle line:84%
you can just wait
later and enter later.

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:39.650 align:middle line:84%
So it might be that your
rivals enter less if you're

00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:41.930 align:middle line:90%
constrained by your debt load.

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:45.500 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so I tend to think of
these as many different stories

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:49.220 align:middle line:84%
in which taking on debt makes
firms soft or makes firms tough,

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:51.980 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, would have
an entry deterring or entry

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:54.770 align:middle line:90%
accommodating effect.

00:11:54.770 --> 00:11:59.720 align:middle line:84%
OK, so this is what Chevalier
is interested in, is basically

00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:03.530 align:middle line:84%
this question of what
effect does debt have,

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:06.470 align:middle line:84%
using the supermarket
industry as an opportunity

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:08.900 align:middle line:90%
to explore debt?

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:11.570 align:middle line:84%
And she takes
advantage of, again,

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:13.020 align:middle line:90%
what's a natural experiment?

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:19.110 align:middle line:84%
Well, Judy claims that the
basic story here, though,

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:21.870 align:middle line:84%
is it's this weird
natural experiment in LBOs

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:25.610 align:middle line:84%
in which there's a family
called the Haft family.

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:28.830 align:middle line:84%
They had a drugstore chain,
the Dart drugstore chain,

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:30.330 align:middle line:90%
and they sold the chain for--

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:32.050 align:middle line:84%
you know, this is
back in the 1990s.

00:12:32.050 --> 00:12:33.925 align:middle line:84%
Businesses were worth
less than they are now.

00:12:33.925 --> 00:12:36.420 align:middle line:84%
They sold their drugstore
chain for $160 million.

00:12:36.420 --> 00:12:39.060 align:middle line:84%
And soon after, they
decided that they

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:40.380 align:middle line:90%
wanted to get into the super--

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:42.570 align:middle line:84%
now, having cashed out of
the drugstore business,

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:44.070 align:middle line:84%
they wanted to get into
the supermarket business.

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:46.050 align:middle line:84%
And they embarked on
a series of attempts

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:51.690 align:middle line:84%
to buy some national supermarket
chain using leveraged buyouts.

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:56.130 align:middle line:84%
So people know what a
leveraged buyout is?

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:58.990 align:middle line:84%
Leveraged buyout is, I
want to buy something.

00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:00.930 align:middle line:84%
I don't have nearly
enough money to do it,

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:03.730 align:middle line:84%
but I just get people to lend
me a tremendous amount of money.

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:05.230 align:middle line:84%
I take all the
borrowed money, and I

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:08.730 align:middle line:84%
use the borrowed money to buy
the business that I want to buy.

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:11.418 align:middle line:84%
And so the leverage
refers to you're

00:13:11.418 --> 00:13:13.710 align:middle line:84%
buying this business largely
with someone else's money,

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:15.627 align:middle line:84%
but you're just taking
on an awful lot of debt

00:13:15.627 --> 00:13:17.680 align:middle line:90%
to buy the business.

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:21.520 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so first thing we know
they did is they tried to buy--

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:23.380 align:middle line:84%
they just started
buying up all the stock

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:25.600 align:middle line:90%
in Safeway on the market.

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:29.110 align:middle line:84%
And then obviously, you
start buying up stock,

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:30.790 align:middle line:84%
and you can buy
up 5% of the stock

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:33.130 align:middle line:84%
of a publicly traded
company before announcing

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:34.220 align:middle line:90%
your intentions.

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:36.330 align:middle line:84%
So they started buying
up stock in Safeway.

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:41.260 align:middle line:84%
They then announced, we want to
do an LBO and take over Safeway.

00:13:41.260 --> 00:13:42.760 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, as soon
as they announced

00:13:42.760 --> 00:13:45.250 align:middle line:84%
that they were going to take
over Safeway, the price of--

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:48.940 align:middle line:84%
like often you buy out
a firm at 50% premium

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:50.833 align:middle line:84%
over the current
stock market price.

00:13:50.833 --> 00:13:53.000 align:middle line:84%
So they announce they're
going to take over Safeway.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:57.190 align:middle line:90%
Price of Safeway stock jumps.

00:13:57.190 --> 00:14:01.738 align:middle line:84%
And then-- so in some sense,
they've bought the stock here.

00:14:01.738 --> 00:14:03.280 align:middle line:84%
They then announce
they're buying it,

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:05.380 align:middle line:90%
and the stock jumps like that.

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:07.450 align:middle line:84%
And then anyway, the
Safeway management

00:14:07.450 --> 00:14:10.840 align:middle line:84%
does not want to be bought
out by the Haft family

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:12.110 align:middle line:90%
and lose their jobs.

00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:14.050 align:middle line:84%
So the Safeway management
decides, no, we also

00:14:14.050 --> 00:14:15.620 align:middle line:90%
think Safeway is undervalued.

00:14:15.620 --> 00:14:18.040 align:middle line:84%
We're going to buy
Safeway out ourselves.

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:20.620 align:middle line:84%
And so then what the
Safeway management did

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:23.710 align:middle line:84%
is they borrowed
several billion dollars,

00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:27.250 align:middle line:84%
did an LBO to buy
the drugstore instead

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:29.020 align:middle line:90%
of letting the Haft family--

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:32.080 align:middle line:84%
sorry, buy Safeway--
the managers themselves

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:33.760 align:middle line:84%
bought the firm
and took it private

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:39.250 align:middle line:84%
rather than letting the
Haft family take it over.

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:42.430 align:middle line:84%
And then I think one thing
that the Haft family noticed

00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:44.590 align:middle line:84%
here was that they made
an awful lot of money

00:14:44.590 --> 00:14:49.870 align:middle line:84%
when the stock did this,
and then after it did this,

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:54.310 align:middle line:84%
then the Safeway management
took it over, and then

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:57.100 align:middle line:84%
had to pay them again to
buy up all of the shares

00:14:57.100 --> 00:14:59.890 align:middle line:84%
that they bought, and to give
up their takeover attempt.

00:14:59.890 --> 00:15:05.300 align:middle line:84%
And so they ended up making $150
million or something like that.

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:07.060 align:middle line:84%
And then what we
don't know is, like,

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:10.580 align:middle line:84%
did the Haft brothers say,
wow, that was awesome.

00:15:10.580 --> 00:15:13.680 align:middle line:84%
I just made $150 million and
I didn't have to do anything,

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:15.972 align:middle line:84%
or did they really want
a supermarket chain?

00:15:15.972 --> 00:15:18.430 align:middle line:84%
But they basically at this
point went from chain, to chain,

00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:21.820 align:middle line:84%
to chain, to chain, to chain,
and kept trying to buy them,

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:23.390 align:middle line:90%
and kept losing.

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:27.730 align:middle line:84%
And the management of all
these different supermarkets

00:15:27.730 --> 00:15:31.132 align:middle line:84%
did either LBOs or what are
called leveraged recaps where

00:15:31.132 --> 00:15:33.340 align:middle line:84%
they just borrowed money,
and didn't take it private,

00:15:33.340 --> 00:15:36.580 align:middle line:84%
but borrowed a lot of money and
bought back a lot of the equity.

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:39.760 align:middle line:84%
And so in this period
of just three years,

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:44.710 align:middle line:84%
19 of the 50 largest supermarket
chains in the US did LBOs.

00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:48.790 align:middle line:84%
And so Judy treats
this as she's going

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:53.350 align:middle line:84%
to think of this as a
relatively random choice.

00:15:53.350 --> 00:15:56.233 align:middle line:84%
Just where did the Haft
brothers go over, and over,

00:15:56.233 --> 00:15:57.650 align:middle line:84%
and over again
trying to buy them?

00:15:57.650 --> 00:16:00.610 align:middle line:84%
And we get this natural
experiment of 19 chains

00:16:00.610 --> 00:16:03.420 align:middle line:84%
doing LBOs, 31 chains
not doing LBOs.

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:06.310 align:middle line:84%
And so you can treat
this as a random event.

00:16:06.310 --> 00:16:10.720 align:middle line:84%
And then the market level
impact of the LBO wave

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:13.090 align:middle line:84%
varied a lot from city to
city because most cities

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:16.830 align:middle line:84%
will have three or four
large supermarket chains.

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:18.080 align:middle line:90%
And these are national chains.

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:19.747 align:middle line:84%
And so it could be
there are some cities

00:16:19.747 --> 00:16:21.997 align:middle line:84%
where all the LBO chains had
almost the entire market.

00:16:21.997 --> 00:16:23.372 align:middle line:84%
There are some
cities where there

00:16:23.372 --> 00:16:25.180 align:middle line:84%
was only one LBO chain,
some cities where

00:16:25.180 --> 00:16:26.970 align:middle line:90%
there were no LBO chains.

00:16:26.970 --> 00:16:30.540 align:middle line:90%


00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:32.140 align:middle line:90%
OK, so what's her dataset?

00:16:32.140 --> 00:16:34.660 align:middle line:84%
It's a few different
data sources.

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:36.450 align:middle line:84%
First, she's got
annual information

00:16:36.450 --> 00:16:40.200 align:middle line:84%
on the number of supermarkets
in each chain, each of 85 MSAs.

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:43.260 align:middle line:90%
MSAs means cities.

00:16:43.260 --> 00:16:46.170 align:middle line:84%
She has the demographics
of the MSAs,

00:16:46.170 --> 00:16:49.230 align:middle line:84%
and how that changed
over the six-year period,

00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:52.710 align:middle line:84%
and then she has whether every
store that was there in 1985

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:59.540 align:middle line:84%
became part of an LBO
firm by 1988 or by 1991.

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:02.750 align:middle line:84%
So she's going to be running a
lot of city-level regressions.

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:06.319 align:middle line:84%
In the city-level regressions,
the main explanatory variable

00:17:06.319 --> 00:17:08.510 align:middle line:90%
is LBO share.

00:17:08.510 --> 00:17:13.670 align:middle line:84%
So LBO share is just the
share of stores in 1985

00:17:13.670 --> 00:17:16.022 align:middle line:90%
that became part of an LBO.

00:17:16.022 --> 00:17:17.730 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going to
be comparing cities

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:19.950 align:middle line:84%
that had lots of
firms doing LBOs

00:17:19.950 --> 00:17:22.160 align:middle line:90%
and not many firms doing LBOs.

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:23.819 align:middle line:84%
And the main thing
she's looking at

00:17:23.819 --> 00:17:30.480 align:middle line:84%
is interest is the effect
of the LBO on entry.

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:32.280 align:middle line:84%
And so she's going
to look at both

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:36.960 align:middle line:84%
do the existing non-LBO firms
expand and build more stores,

00:17:36.960 --> 00:17:40.030 align:middle line:84%
like a Cournot-like model
that we can't expand,

00:17:40.030 --> 00:17:42.010 align:middle line:84%
therefore they start
building more stores,

00:17:42.010 --> 00:17:44.310 align:middle line:84%
and then she also looks
at effects on entry

00:17:44.310 --> 00:17:48.180 align:middle line:84%
by brand new firms
come into this market,

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:51.720 align:middle line:84%
perhaps because they know that
Safeway can no longer expand,

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:53.610 align:middle line:84%
or they expect Safeway
to raise its prices,

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:54.700 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:57.360 align:middle line:84%
So she's going to look
at effects on expansions

00:17:57.360 --> 00:17:58.385 align:middle line:90%
and on new entry.

00:17:58.385 --> 00:18:02.510 align:middle line:90%


00:18:02.510 --> 00:18:06.320 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, it becomes, compared
to many papers we've looked at,

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:08.690 align:middle line:84%
fairly simple paper
econometrically.

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:11.750 align:middle line:84%
It's just largely a
lot of regressions,

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:16.042 align:middle line:84%
looking at how conditions
in the market change when--

00:18:16.042 --> 00:18:18.500 align:middle line:84%
differ between the cities that
have lots of LBOs and cities

00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:20.090 align:middle line:90%
that don't have LBOs.

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:25.640 align:middle line:84%
So the first one here is there
is some fairly weak evidence

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:29.870 align:middle line:84%
that number of early
LBOs led to an increase

00:18:29.870 --> 00:18:31.860 align:middle line:84%
in the total number of
stores in the market.

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:34.700 align:middle line:84%
So you can see that that's
this coefficient here.

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:37.400 align:middle line:84%
So it's regressing like
log of the number of stores

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:39.720 align:middle line:84%
on a market on a
bunch of things.

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:42.020 align:middle line:84%
And then one of
the things is the--

00:18:42.020 --> 00:18:51.195 align:middle line:84%
and this is log of stores as of
1991 on market characteristics.

00:18:51.195 --> 00:18:52.570 align:middle line:84%
And this one here
is the fraction

00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:56.920 align:middle line:84%
of stores that had done
LBOs between 1995 and 1998.

00:18:56.920 --> 00:19:01.870 align:middle line:84%
And what it's showing is
that in the cities that

00:19:01.870 --> 00:19:07.360 align:middle line:84%
have more LBO stores, more
stores that did LBOs by 1998,

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:11.050 align:middle line:84%
you see more stores
in the market in 2001.

00:19:11.050 --> 00:19:14.680 align:middle line:84%
So you would think that's
consistent with this story

00:19:14.680 --> 00:19:17.440 align:middle line:84%
that LBOs soften
price competition.

00:19:17.440 --> 00:19:20.410 align:middle line:84%
Safeway after the LBO is
going to raise its prices

00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:25.450 align:middle line:84%
to try to eat up its customer
loyalty and pay off its debts.

00:19:25.450 --> 00:19:27.130 align:middle line:84%
Other firms are
going to recognize

00:19:27.130 --> 00:19:29.080 align:middle line:84%
that as that prices are going
to be higher in that market.

00:19:29.080 --> 00:19:31.305 align:middle line:84%
We could move in and enter
there, or expand there.

00:19:31.305 --> 00:19:37.930 align:middle line:90%


00:19:37.930 --> 00:19:43.000 align:middle line:84%
So that was total
stores in the market.

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:50.980 align:middle line:84%
So this is a question of when
there are more LBO firms,

00:19:50.980 --> 00:19:56.410 align:middle line:84%
do the non-LBO firms build
more stores in the market?

00:19:56.410 --> 00:19:59.090 align:middle line:90%
So this is coefficient here.

00:19:59.090 --> 00:20:03.610 align:middle line:84%
If you at the number of stores
built by non-LBO incumbents,

00:20:03.610 --> 00:20:09.490 align:middle line:84%
when you have an early LBO, the
non-LBO incumbents expand more.

00:20:09.490 --> 00:20:12.880 align:middle line:84%
Again, it's not highly
significant effect,

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:18.460 align:middle line:84%
but non-LBO
incumbents expand more

00:20:18.460 --> 00:20:20.850 align:middle line:84%
when there are a lot of early
LBO firms in the market.

00:20:20.850 --> 00:20:27.010 align:middle line:90%


00:20:27.010 --> 00:20:30.250 align:middle line:84%
Third set of regressions
is about de novo entry.

00:20:30.250 --> 00:20:34.960 align:middle line:84%
So de novo entry is looking at
do you see brand new chains that

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:37.120 align:middle line:84%
were never operating in
this market before start

00:20:37.120 --> 00:20:38.480 align:middle line:90%
to open in stores?

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:41.320 align:middle line:84%
And typically big national
chains, they either

00:20:41.320 --> 00:20:43.570 align:middle line:84%
have a lot of stores in a
city or no stores in a city.

00:20:43.570 --> 00:20:45.612 align:middle line:84%
Because if you're going
to have stores in a city,

00:20:45.612 --> 00:20:47.450 align:middle line:84%
you need warehouses
and things like that.

00:20:47.450 --> 00:20:49.420 align:middle line:84%
And then occasionally,
the big chains

00:20:49.420 --> 00:20:51.820 align:middle line:84%
will decide, OK, now we're
moving into this metro area,

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:53.920 align:middle line:90%
and they'll build out there.

00:20:53.920 --> 00:20:55.750 align:middle line:84%
Also at the time,
you were getting

00:20:55.750 --> 00:20:59.410 align:middle line:84%
Kmart and Walmart were
expanding from just other things

00:20:59.410 --> 00:21:01.690 align:middle line:90%
into groceries.

00:21:01.690 --> 00:21:04.480 align:middle line:90%
And so what they find on--

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:07.150 align:middle line:84%
what she finds on
de novo entry is,

00:21:07.150 --> 00:21:09.050 align:middle line:90%
again, this coefficient here.

00:21:09.050 --> 00:21:13.478 align:middle line:84%
The more early LBOs there are
in a city, the more likely

00:21:13.478 --> 00:21:15.520 align:middle line:84%
it is that we're going to
see some new chain that

00:21:15.520 --> 00:21:18.670 align:middle line:84%
wasn't in the city, or
Kmart, or Walmart opening up

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:22.170 align:middle line:90%
new supermarkets there.

00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:25.650 align:middle line:84%
So I guess, overall,
these results

00:21:25.650 --> 00:21:31.830 align:middle line:84%
are consistent with models in
which LBOs make incumbents soft.

00:21:31.830 --> 00:21:34.760 align:middle line:84%
This could be-- what the
soft is in terms of entry,

00:21:34.760 --> 00:21:37.350 align:middle line:84%
it could be the LBO
firms are raising prices,

00:21:37.350 --> 00:21:40.230 align:middle line:84%
or it could be that the LBO
firms are capacity constrained.

00:21:40.230 --> 00:21:43.530 align:middle line:84%
And that leads the rivals
to introduce more stores.

00:21:43.530 --> 00:21:46.710 align:middle line:84%
Certainly, if it's the
raising prices part,

00:21:46.710 --> 00:21:50.070 align:middle line:84%
the raising prices one
would be, that is--

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:53.310 align:middle line:84%
you do want to be soft in
price competition games.

00:21:53.310 --> 00:21:55.770 align:middle line:84%
So if it's a commitment
to raise your prices that

00:21:55.770 --> 00:21:59.100 align:middle line:84%
gets your rivals to raise
their prices, that's good.

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:02.910 align:middle line:84%
This isn't to say
that you do more--

00:22:02.910 --> 00:22:04.950 align:middle line:84%
this isn't to say that
strategic effect of LBOs

00:22:04.950 --> 00:22:06.585 align:middle line:84%
is what makes you
do an LBO, but it

00:22:06.585 --> 00:22:08.460 align:middle line:84%
is saying that it's one
of the considerations

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:10.140 align:middle line:84%
in whether to take
an LBO is-- one

00:22:10.140 --> 00:22:12.240 align:middle line:84%
of the benefits the
LBO would be providing

00:22:12.240 --> 00:22:14.670 align:middle line:84%
is the committing to
raise your price, which

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:16.295 align:middle line:84%
gets your rivals to
raise their prices.

00:22:16.295 --> 00:22:18.628 align:middle line:84%
And that would be one of the
factors in the calculation.

00:22:18.628 --> 00:22:20.550 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, you
would do more LBOs

00:22:20.550 --> 00:22:23.850 align:middle line:84%
than you would in the
open loop LBO equilibrium

00:22:23.850 --> 00:22:26.430 align:middle line:84%
where the rivals didn't know
that you were doing the LBO

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:27.950 align:middle line:84%
and it couldn't
affect your pricing.

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:30.880 align:middle line:90%


00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:33.000 align:middle line:90%
Questions on that?

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:36.990 align:middle line:84%
And then Judy also has
a nice follow-up paper

00:22:36.990 --> 00:22:41.340 align:middle line:84%
in the Journal of Finance which
looks directly at pricing.

00:22:41.340 --> 00:22:48.010 align:middle line:84%
So this one, again,
it's a fairly--

00:22:48.010 --> 00:22:50.435 align:middle line:84%
she's gotten data from
supermarket scanner data,

00:22:50.435 --> 00:22:52.060 align:middle line:84%
and looking at
supermarket scanner data

00:22:52.060 --> 00:22:54.580 align:middle line:84%
to compare how the firms
with undertaken LBOs

00:22:54.580 --> 00:22:56.750 align:middle line:84%
priced compared
to non-LBO firms.

00:22:56.750 --> 00:22:59.500 align:middle line:84%
And what she finds
is that by 1992,

00:22:59.500 --> 00:23:04.610 align:middle line:84%
the firms that did LBOs have
raised their prices by 2% to 3%.

00:23:04.610 --> 00:23:08.380 align:middle line:90%


00:23:08.380 --> 00:23:12.790 align:middle line:84%
In markets where there isn't
some dominant firm that

00:23:12.790 --> 00:23:16.480 align:middle line:84%
didn't do an LBO, then
market level prices

00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:18.770 align:middle line:90%
typically go up after the LBO.

00:23:18.770 --> 00:23:24.580 align:middle line:84%
But in markets where you do have
the dominant low debt firm, what

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:27.370 align:middle line:84%
we see is that actually, on
average in the market prices,

00:23:27.370 --> 00:23:29.510 align:middle line:84%
if the market level
prices go down,

00:23:29.510 --> 00:23:33.070 align:middle line:84%
it seems like sometimes
the low debt firms

00:23:33.070 --> 00:23:35.140 align:middle line:90%
are exploiting the LBO firms.

00:23:35.140 --> 00:23:37.600 align:middle line:84%
And the LBO firms are
committing to cut prices,

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:39.190 align:middle line:84%
and the LBO firms
perhaps could be

00:23:39.190 --> 00:23:42.520 align:middle line:84%
deciding this is an opportunity
for us to keep our prices low.

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:46.490 align:middle line:84%
And we see some increased
exits of LBO stores

00:23:46.490 --> 00:23:48.830 align:middle line:84%
where the LBO stores find
it difficult to compete

00:23:48.830 --> 00:23:50.410 align:middle line:90%
with the non-LBO firms.

00:23:50.410 --> 00:23:54.270 align:middle line:90%


00:23:54.270 --> 00:23:55.440 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

00:23:55.440 --> 00:23:57.900 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it's two
nice papers that

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:00.062 align:middle line:84%
are very much of
that first approach

00:24:00.062 --> 00:24:01.770 align:middle line:84%
I said of just looking
at the investment,

00:24:01.770 --> 00:24:03.210 align:middle line:84%
seeing what the effect
of the investment is.

00:24:03.210 --> 00:24:04.252 align:middle line:90%
What does it do to firms?

00:24:04.252 --> 00:24:07.810 align:middle line:90%


00:24:07.810 --> 00:24:12.050 align:middle line:84%
OK, so then the second paper
I wanted to talk about,

00:24:12.050 --> 00:24:18.340 align:middle line:84%
this is a paper Sara and I
wrote about entity deterrence

00:24:18.340 --> 00:24:20.680 align:middle line:84%
in the pharmaceutical
industries.

00:24:20.680 --> 00:24:25.000 align:middle line:84%
So I guess basics
of pharmaceuticals

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:30.010 align:middle line:90%
is, you invent a new drug.

00:24:30.010 --> 00:24:31.430 align:middle line:90%
You patent the new drug.

00:24:31.430 --> 00:24:32.830 align:middle line:84%
After you patent
the drug, you do

00:24:32.830 --> 00:24:34.480 align:middle line:84%
some number of years
of clinical trials

00:24:34.480 --> 00:24:37.825 align:middle line:84%
to prove that the drug
is safe and effective.

00:24:37.825 --> 00:24:39.700 align:middle line:84%
If you have enough, you
present your evidence

00:24:39.700 --> 00:24:42.260 align:middle line:84%
that drug is safe and
effective to the FDA.

00:24:42.260 --> 00:24:46.300 align:middle line:84%
They approve your drug for
sale in the United States.

00:24:46.300 --> 00:24:49.510 align:middle line:84%
The clock has been
ticking since you

00:24:49.510 --> 00:24:51.610 align:middle line:84%
patented the drug
on how long you will

00:24:51.610 --> 00:24:53.480 align:middle line:90%
have patent protection over it.

00:24:53.480 --> 00:24:55.720 align:middle line:84%
There are various
more complicated rules

00:24:55.720 --> 00:24:59.590 align:middle line:84%
about if it's taken you this
long to get the drug approved,

00:24:59.590 --> 00:25:01.650 align:middle line:84%
you can get extensions
to your patent term.

00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:04.150 align:middle line:84%
And there are also various ways
you can get other extensions

00:25:04.150 --> 00:25:04.983 align:middle line:90%
to your patent term.

00:25:04.983 --> 00:25:07.345 align:middle line:84%
But all drugs will have
some date at which--

00:25:07.345 --> 00:25:10.840 align:middle line:90%


00:25:10.840 --> 00:25:14.590 align:middle line:84%
they'll have some date
at which the price drops.

00:25:14.590 --> 00:25:18.520 align:middle line:84%
Prices of many drugs
are quite high.

00:25:18.520 --> 00:25:21.560 align:middle line:84%
I think these days, most
people have insurance.

00:25:21.560 --> 00:25:24.790 align:middle line:84%
But because most
people have insurance,

00:25:24.790 --> 00:25:27.482 align:middle line:84%
many new drugs come out that
treat high blood pressure,

00:25:27.482 --> 00:25:28.940 align:middle line:84%
or cholesterol
reduction, whatever,

00:25:28.940 --> 00:25:31.690 align:middle line:84%
and they come out with
prices of $1,000 a month,

00:25:31.690 --> 00:25:34.730 align:middle line:90%
or $2,000 a month, or whatever.

00:25:34.730 --> 00:25:38.570 align:middle line:84%
And then obviously, you
have insurance as a copay.

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:41.860 align:middle line:84%
But the firms are getting these
very high $2,000 a month prices

00:25:41.860 --> 00:25:42.650 align:middle line:90%
for new drugs.

00:25:42.650 --> 00:25:44.775 align:middle line:84%
Now, sometimes people are
looking at pricing things

00:25:44.775 --> 00:25:46.870 align:middle line:90%
at $50,000 a month.

00:25:46.870 --> 00:25:49.720 align:middle line:84%
At some point, the
patent expires.

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:53.620 align:middle line:84%
When the patent expires,
there's something

00:25:53.620 --> 00:25:56.498 align:middle line:84%
called an abbreviated
new drug approval, ANDA.

00:25:56.498 --> 00:25:57.915 align:middle line:84%
I think that's
what it stands for.

00:25:57.915 --> 00:26:00.610 align:middle line:90%


00:26:00.610 --> 00:26:03.700 align:middle line:84%
Rivals can come in,
show that they're

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:05.320 align:middle line:84%
producing a chemical
compound that's

00:26:05.320 --> 00:26:07.570 align:middle line:84%
equivalent to the chemical
compound being produced

00:26:07.570 --> 00:26:12.670 align:middle line:84%
by the branded drug manufacturer
that was approved by the FDA.

00:26:12.670 --> 00:26:15.010 align:middle line:84%
Earlier in US history,
you had to again prove

00:26:15.010 --> 00:26:17.135 align:middle line:84%
safety and effectiveness,
and there was essentially

00:26:17.135 --> 00:26:17.870 align:middle line:90%
no generic entry.

00:26:17.870 --> 00:26:22.120 align:middle line:84%
Now, all you have to do is prove
bioequivalence of your drug

00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:23.540 align:middle line:90%
with the existing drug.

00:26:23.540 --> 00:26:27.310 align:middle line:84%
Then you can come in and
compete with the existing firm.

00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:30.130 align:middle line:84%
Typically, what happens is
firms are going along charging

00:26:30.130 --> 00:26:34.450 align:middle line:84%
their $1,000 a month price,
and then know, generics enter.

00:26:34.450 --> 00:26:36.962 align:middle line:90%
And generics just come in and--

00:26:36.962 --> 00:26:38.920 align:middle line:84%
there's some date the
generic entry is allowed.

00:26:38.920 --> 00:26:41.410 align:middle line:84%
And the generic just
goes like that and prices

00:26:41.410 --> 00:26:44.150 align:middle line:84%
at a small fraction of what
the branded firm charges.

00:26:44.150 --> 00:26:47.725 align:middle line:84%
Strangely, the branded firm
does not cut its price to match.

00:26:47.725 --> 00:26:50.350 align:middle line:84%
Typically, what the branded firm
does, if anything, is do this,

00:26:50.350 --> 00:26:52.300 align:middle line:90%
and price even higher.

00:26:52.300 --> 00:26:56.800 align:middle line:84%
Because now, all they're serving
is the people who will not buy--

00:26:56.800 --> 00:27:00.340 align:middle line:84%
people who will not buy a
bioequivalent drug, and so

00:27:00.340 --> 00:27:02.260 align:middle line:84%
those people you might
think are captive

00:27:02.260 --> 00:27:04.680 align:middle line:84%
and are not going to leave
no matter what you charge.

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:06.730 align:middle line:84%
But in any case, if
you look at the market

00:27:06.730 --> 00:27:09.982 align:middle line:84%
shares of the branded drug,
the market shares go like this.

00:27:09.982 --> 00:27:11.690 align:middle line:84%
The branded drug has
a market share of 1,

00:27:11.690 --> 00:27:14.950 align:middle line:84%
and then it pretty quickly
asymptotes to some very small

00:27:14.950 --> 00:27:16.220 align:middle line:90%
number, like 10%.

00:27:16.220 --> 00:27:18.280 align:middle line:84%
And then the generics
go like this,

00:27:18.280 --> 00:27:21.480 align:middle line:84%
and then they start to
survey the whole market.

00:27:21.480 --> 00:27:28.720 align:middle line:84%
So what's nice about
drugs as a place

00:27:28.720 --> 00:27:31.810 align:middle line:84%
to study strategic
investment is there's

00:27:31.810 --> 00:27:34.280 align:middle line:84%
no entry possible
until some date,

00:27:34.280 --> 00:27:36.530 align:middle line:84%
and then there's some date
on which entry is possible.

00:27:36.530 --> 00:27:39.460 align:middle line:84%
And looking at the FDA
data, you know the date

00:27:39.460 --> 00:27:41.630 align:middle line:90%
at which the entry is possible.

00:27:41.630 --> 00:27:44.770 align:middle line:84%
And so we get this opportunity
where there's no entry,

00:27:44.770 --> 00:27:46.400 align:middle line:84%
no entry possible,
no entry possible.

00:27:46.400 --> 00:27:48.130 align:middle line:84%
Suddenly entry is
possible, and you

00:27:48.130 --> 00:27:51.610 align:middle line:84%
can use that change in how firms
behave around the time entry

00:27:51.610 --> 00:27:54.040 align:middle line:84%
becomes possible to
identify what firms

00:27:54.040 --> 00:27:55.395 align:middle line:90%
might be doing to deter entry.

00:27:55.395 --> 00:27:58.330 align:middle line:90%


00:27:58.330 --> 00:28:01.000 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, our data
sample in this paper,

00:28:01.000 --> 00:28:04.960 align:middle line:84%
it's 63 branded drugs that lost
patent protection at some point

00:28:04.960 --> 00:28:11.160 align:middle line:90%
between 1986 and 1992.

00:28:11.160 --> 00:28:13.360 align:middle line:90%
I think I said that already.

00:28:13.360 --> 00:28:19.280 align:middle line:84%
So this paper is, as I said,
the reduced form approach.

00:28:19.280 --> 00:28:22.780 align:middle line:84%
We're trying to understand
not what the incentives are.

00:28:22.780 --> 00:28:26.380 align:middle line:84%
We think that in many cases,
the economics is simple enough

00:28:26.380 --> 00:28:29.320 align:middle line:84%
that we know what the incentives
for pharmaceutical manufacturers

00:28:29.320 --> 00:28:32.770 align:middle line:84%
are to do, and how
they would deter entry.

00:28:32.770 --> 00:28:35.980 align:middle line:84%
The question is, are they
reacting to those incentives

00:28:35.980 --> 00:28:39.070 align:middle line:84%
to deter entry, and do we
see them taking actions

00:28:39.070 --> 00:28:42.370 align:middle line:84%
to try to keep generic
drugs out of their markets?

00:28:42.370 --> 00:28:44.320 align:middle line:84%
And the way we
propose to do this

00:28:44.320 --> 00:28:47.987 align:middle line:84%
is to identify a prediction
that we think differs

00:28:47.987 --> 00:28:50.320 align:middle line:84%
between the strategic model
and the non-strategic model,

00:28:50.320 --> 00:28:52.420 align:middle line:90%
and then test that prediction.

00:28:52.420 --> 00:28:56.320 align:middle line:84%
So the prediction comes
off two observations.

00:28:56.320 --> 00:29:00.190 align:middle line:84%
First observation is that
strategic entry deterrence

00:29:00.190 --> 00:29:05.170 align:middle line:84%
models often predict that the
relationship between investment

00:29:05.170 --> 00:29:08.890 align:middle line:84%
and market size is going
to be non monotonic.

00:29:08.890 --> 00:29:16.550 align:middle line:84%
The idea is that if you're Eli
Lilly and your patent on Prozac

00:29:16.550 --> 00:29:20.240 align:middle line:84%
is expiring, or if you're Pfizer
and your patent on Lipitor

00:29:20.240 --> 00:29:22.820 align:middle line:84%
is expiring, there's
nothing you can do

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:24.650 align:middle line:90%
to stop entry from occurring.

00:29:24.650 --> 00:29:28.460 align:middle line:84%
Because Lipitor is bringing
in billions of dollars

00:29:28.460 --> 00:29:29.430 align:middle line:90%
a year in profits.

00:29:29.430 --> 00:29:31.920 align:middle line:90%
It costs almost nothing to make.

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:33.810 align:middle line:84%
As soon as the patent
on Lipitor expires,

00:29:33.810 --> 00:29:37.970 align:middle line:84%
there's going to be a ton of
generic atorvastatin firms.

00:29:37.970 --> 00:29:41.670 align:middle line:84%
There's nothing that
Pfizer could do about that.

00:29:41.670 --> 00:29:43.820 align:middle line:90%
Most drugs in the data--

00:29:43.820 --> 00:29:46.320 align:middle line:84%
we think of drugs as having
these high prices and incredibly

00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:47.257 align:middle line:90%
high revenues.

00:29:47.257 --> 00:29:48.840 align:middle line:84%
If you actually look
at the full cross

00:29:48.840 --> 00:29:51.810 align:middle line:84%
section of FDA approved
drugs with expiring patents,

00:29:51.810 --> 00:29:54.000 align:middle line:90%
most of them don't do anything.

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:56.380 align:middle line:90%
Firms get drugs.

00:29:56.380 --> 00:29:58.150 align:middle line:90%
They get the drugs approved.

00:29:58.150 --> 00:30:00.760 align:middle line:84%
But then the drug gets
approved, but it's not

00:30:00.760 --> 00:30:03.190 align:middle line:84%
as good as some other
drug in the market.

00:30:03.190 --> 00:30:06.710 align:middle line:84%
Or it was the best drug
when it was first patented,

00:30:06.710 --> 00:30:09.220 align:middle line:84%
but then some new drug has
come in and supplanted it.

00:30:09.220 --> 00:30:12.040 align:middle line:84%
So many, many drugs, basically,
to first order approximation,

00:30:12.040 --> 00:30:13.300 align:middle line:90%
nobody takes.

00:30:13.300 --> 00:30:15.010 align:middle line:84%
And if you have a drug
that nobody takes,

00:30:15.010 --> 00:30:16.802 align:middle line:84%
there's never going to
be any generic entry

00:30:16.802 --> 00:30:20.770 align:middle line:84%
because the generic firm is not
going to come into a market that

00:30:20.770 --> 00:30:22.600 align:middle line:84%
already has no
patients when it's got

00:30:22.600 --> 00:30:24.410 align:middle line:90%
pharmaceutical reps pushing it.

00:30:24.410 --> 00:30:28.210 align:middle line:84%
And so our thought on the paper
is that these entry deterrence

00:30:28.210 --> 00:30:30.700 align:middle line:84%
incentives really are
something that exist just

00:30:30.700 --> 00:30:32.560 align:middle line:84%
in intermediate sized
markets, and markets

00:30:32.560 --> 00:30:35.850 align:middle line:84%
that are big enough so that
generic firms would want

00:30:35.850 --> 00:30:38.970 align:middle line:84%
to bother, but small enough that
it's plausible that you could

00:30:38.970 --> 00:30:40.710 align:middle line:84%
keep the generic
firms out by taking

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:42.430 align:middle line:90%
some set of strategic actions.

00:30:42.430 --> 00:30:46.530 align:middle line:84%
And so there's this potential
non-monotonic effect of there

00:30:46.530 --> 00:30:48.130 align:middle line:90%
will be some action.

00:30:48.130 --> 00:30:50.670 align:middle line:84%
And in small markets,
you don't do this action.

00:30:50.670 --> 00:30:52.540 align:middle line:84%
And in big markets,
you do this action.

00:30:52.540 --> 00:30:56.470 align:middle line:84%
And then in really big markets,
you don't do the action again.

00:30:56.470 --> 00:31:01.230 align:middle line:84%
And then the other observation
is that non-strategic models--

00:31:01.230 --> 00:31:05.910 align:middle line:84%
so if I'm putting here
market size on this,

00:31:05.910 --> 00:31:10.420 align:middle line:84%
and investment on this
axis, the other observation

00:31:10.420 --> 00:31:15.070 align:middle line:84%
is that non-strategic models
like the model ignoring

00:31:15.070 --> 00:31:17.350 align:middle line:84%
that entry deterrence
incentives often

00:31:17.350 --> 00:31:19.660 align:middle line:84%
predict that the relationship
between market size

00:31:19.660 --> 00:31:21.940 align:middle line:84%
and investment is
going to be monotone.

00:31:21.940 --> 00:31:25.690 align:middle line:84%
We talk in the paper about there
being basically two effects that

00:31:25.690 --> 00:31:29.080 align:middle line:84%
determine whether we should
see more or less investments

00:31:29.080 --> 00:31:31.300 align:middle line:90%
in big markets.

00:31:31.300 --> 00:31:33.865 align:middle line:84%
One is the direct
effect, and it's just,

00:31:33.865 --> 00:31:38.530 align:middle line:84%
is a marginal investment
return larger in a big market

00:31:38.530 --> 00:31:39.980 align:middle line:90%
than it is in a small market?

00:31:39.980 --> 00:31:42.640 align:middle line:84%
And many investments
like TV commercials,

00:31:42.640 --> 00:31:46.900 align:middle line:84%
like you may be aware that
there's just-- if you watch TV,

00:31:46.900 --> 00:31:49.120 align:middle line:84%
there's zillions of these
annoying commercials that

00:31:49.120 --> 00:31:51.295 align:middle line:84%
go on for a minute talking
about all the conditions

00:31:51.295 --> 00:31:53.920 align:middle line:84%
that someone might have, and all
the side effects of this drug,

00:31:53.920 --> 00:31:59.320 align:middle line:84%
and buy Xeljanz or whatever
at the end of the commercial.

00:31:59.320 --> 00:32:02.477 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, commercials
like that are only

00:32:02.477 --> 00:32:04.810 align:middle line:84%
going to be feasible for
conditions that a lot of people

00:32:04.810 --> 00:32:05.418 align:middle line:90%
have.

00:32:05.418 --> 00:32:07.960 align:middle line:84%
If there's some condition that's
very rare in the population,

00:32:07.960 --> 00:32:10.100 align:middle line:84%
you can't buy a
national TV commercial

00:32:10.100 --> 00:32:11.195 align:middle line:90%
advertising your drug.

00:32:11.195 --> 00:32:13.070 align:middle line:84%
If there's a condition
that many people have,

00:32:13.070 --> 00:32:14.420 align:middle line:90%
you can buy the commercial.

00:32:14.420 --> 00:32:18.855 align:middle line:84%
So we'd call that the direct
effect of investment returns.

00:32:18.855 --> 00:32:20.480 align:middle line:84%
The more people who
have the condition,

00:32:20.480 --> 00:32:25.720 align:middle line:84%
the more you're going to invest
in over-the-air TV advertising.

00:32:25.720 --> 00:32:30.430 align:middle line:84%
A second effect-- do I
have second effect here?--

00:32:30.430 --> 00:32:32.470 align:middle line:90%
strategic effect.

00:32:32.470 --> 00:32:35.860 align:middle line:84%
The second strategic
effect is, are the returns

00:32:35.860 --> 00:32:38.500 align:middle line:84%
to this investment bigger
in a monopoly market,

00:32:38.500 --> 00:32:40.240 align:middle line:90%
or in a duopoly market?

00:32:40.240 --> 00:32:45.970 align:middle line:84%
Because the bigger is the
market, the more likely

00:32:45.970 --> 00:32:48.108 align:middle line:84%
you are to end up
with competition.

00:32:48.108 --> 00:32:49.900 align:middle line:84%
And so you're going to
have to think about,

00:32:49.900 --> 00:32:52.390 align:middle line:84%
is this an investment that
I'm making more valuable when

00:32:52.390 --> 00:32:54.040 align:middle line:84%
there's competition,
or more value when

00:32:54.040 --> 00:32:55.780 align:middle line:90%
there's no competition?

00:32:55.780 --> 00:32:58.817 align:middle line:84%
So if those two effects
go in the same way--

00:32:58.817 --> 00:33:01.150 align:middle line:84%
this is something you want
to invest more in big markets

00:33:01.150 --> 00:33:02.590 align:middle line:84%
than small markets,
and this thing

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:04.990 align:middle line:84%
you want to invest more
in oligopoly markets

00:33:04.990 --> 00:33:06.520 align:middle line:90%
than in monopoly markets--

00:33:06.520 --> 00:33:08.800 align:middle line:84%
then we're going to see
a monotone relationship

00:33:08.800 --> 00:33:11.170 align:middle line:84%
between market size
and the investment.

00:33:11.170 --> 00:33:13.570 align:middle line:84%
And so if you have
investments like that,

00:33:13.570 --> 00:33:15.500 align:middle line:84%
we think those two effects
go in the same way,

00:33:15.500 --> 00:33:16.875 align:middle line:84%
we would expect
it to be monotone

00:33:16.875 --> 00:33:18.808 align:middle line:84%
if there wasn't the
entry deterrence motive.

00:33:18.808 --> 00:33:20.350 align:middle line:84%
But with the entry
deterrence motive,

00:33:20.350 --> 00:33:24.700 align:middle line:84%
we could look for a
non-monotone pattern.

00:33:24.700 --> 00:33:27.770 align:middle line:84%
I think I have several
more slides on this.

00:33:27.770 --> 00:33:29.570 align:middle line:90%
I'll see how many of them I do.

00:33:29.570 --> 00:33:33.920 align:middle line:84%
So here would be an example
of a simple numerical model

00:33:33.920 --> 00:33:40.490 align:middle line:84%
I can solve that has this
property, monotone effect

00:33:40.490 --> 00:33:41.960 align:middle line:84%
without entry
deterrence motives,

00:33:41.960 --> 00:33:44.460 align:middle line:84%
and a non-monotone with the
entry deterrence motives.

00:33:44.460 --> 00:33:47.540 align:middle line:84%
So here, think about a
cross section of markets,

00:33:47.540 --> 00:33:51.020 align:middle line:84%
and the markets are identical
other than the number of people,

00:33:51.020 --> 00:33:52.520 align:middle line:90%
zi, who--

00:33:52.520 --> 00:33:55.220 align:middle line:84%
you can think of this as the
market for a particular drug,

00:33:55.220 --> 00:34:00.250 align:middle line:84%
and zi is the number of people
who would be served by the drug.

00:34:00.250 --> 00:34:03.640 align:middle line:84%
Assume that each
market has a continuum

00:34:03.640 --> 00:34:08.199 align:middle line:84%
of types with theta distributed
uniformly on 0 and 1.

00:34:08.199 --> 00:34:13.280 align:middle line:84%
Assume that this advertising
has this technology where

00:34:13.280 --> 00:34:18.440 align:middle line:84%
A is my per-consumer
spending on the advertising.

00:34:18.440 --> 00:34:22.190 align:middle line:84%
And if I spend A per
consumer on the advertising,

00:34:22.190 --> 00:34:27.250 align:middle line:84%
then consumers are going to
get utility theta times root 2A

00:34:27.250 --> 00:34:30.429 align:middle line:84%
minus p1 if they buy
the branded drug,

00:34:30.429 --> 00:34:34.530 align:middle line:84%
1/2 theta root 2A minus p if
they buy the generic drug,

00:34:34.530 --> 00:34:38.380 align:middle line:84%
my rival, and 0 if
they buy neither good.

00:34:38.380 --> 00:34:40.150 align:middle line:84%
So a motivation
for this technology

00:34:40.150 --> 00:34:46.060 align:middle line:84%
would be some drugs treat
diseases that are typically

00:34:46.060 --> 00:34:48.760 align:middle line:90%
managed by specialists.

00:34:48.760 --> 00:34:51.850 align:middle line:84%
The way you market drugs that
are sold to specialists, rather

00:34:51.850 --> 00:34:54.940 align:middle line:84%
than putting on TV commercials,
you just send sales reps

00:34:54.940 --> 00:34:56.060 align:middle line:90%
to the specialists.

00:34:56.060 --> 00:34:58.480 align:middle line:84%
So if this is a not
very common disease,

00:34:58.480 --> 00:35:02.050 align:middle line:84%
you have the top 25 specialists
in this disease in the Boston

00:35:02.050 --> 00:35:02.620 align:middle line:90%
area.

00:35:02.620 --> 00:35:05.390 align:middle line:84%
You send a sales rep to
visit every one of them.

00:35:05.390 --> 00:35:08.200 align:middle line:84%
You tell them one on one about
the drug and all the newest

00:35:08.200 --> 00:35:10.600 align:middle line:84%
data on its
effectiveness, and you try

00:35:10.600 --> 00:35:12.400 align:middle line:90%
to get them to prescribe it.

00:35:12.400 --> 00:35:15.970 align:middle line:84%
So we're modeling that as the
more you spend on advertising,

00:35:15.970 --> 00:35:18.740 align:middle line:84%
the higher utility people will
have for buying your drug.

00:35:18.740 --> 00:35:21.340 align:middle line:84%
But because the doctors
you're talking to

00:35:21.340 --> 00:35:23.920 align:middle line:84%
will know that the
generic is also

00:35:23.920 --> 00:35:26.350 align:middle line:84%
much the same in
this model, I'm going

00:35:26.350 --> 00:35:28.100 align:middle line:84%
to assume that
people get utility--

00:35:28.100 --> 00:35:29.860 align:middle line:84%
when you raise
people's willingness

00:35:29.860 --> 00:35:32.120 align:middle line:84%
to pay for your drug, you
also raise their willingness

00:35:32.120 --> 00:35:34.250 align:middle line:90%
to pay for the generic drug.

00:35:34.250 --> 00:35:38.104 align:middle line:90%
What happens in this model--

00:35:38.104 --> 00:35:38.812 align:middle line:90%
give me a second.

00:35:38.812 --> 00:35:39.771 align:middle line:90%
It's right here or not.

00:35:39.771 --> 00:35:40.430 align:middle line:90%
Nope, OK.

00:35:40.430 --> 00:35:42.020 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, what happens
in this model

00:35:42.020 --> 00:35:44.000 align:middle line:84%
I will explain it a
little more later.

00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:47.210 align:middle line:84%
If there's no strategic
entry deterrence motive,

00:35:47.210 --> 00:35:52.390 align:middle line:84%
this is a model where you would
do less investing the larger is

00:35:52.390 --> 00:35:52.905 align:middle line:90%
the market.

00:35:52.905 --> 00:35:55.030 align:middle line:84%
And you're going to do less
investing the larger is

00:35:55.030 --> 00:35:58.330 align:middle line:84%
the market because the larger
is the market, the more likely

00:35:58.330 --> 00:36:00.670 align:middle line:84%
it is that the
generic competitor is

00:36:00.670 --> 00:36:02.180 align:middle line:90%
going to end up entering.

00:36:02.180 --> 00:36:04.360 align:middle line:84%
If the generic competitor
ends up entering,

00:36:04.360 --> 00:36:08.230 align:middle line:84%
it's as if the gain you're
getting is 1/2 theta root 2A

00:36:08.230 --> 00:36:10.750 align:middle line:90%
instead of theta root 2A.

00:36:10.750 --> 00:36:13.263 align:middle line:84%
Because it's only
the marginal way

00:36:13.263 --> 00:36:15.430 align:middle line:84%
that increases your value
relative to the competitor

00:36:15.430 --> 00:36:16.130 align:middle line:90%
that matters.

00:36:16.130 --> 00:36:19.300 align:middle line:84%
So the bigger is the market,
the more likely generics

00:36:19.300 --> 00:36:24.260 align:middle line:90%
are going to enter and profit.

00:36:24.260 --> 00:36:26.140 align:middle line:84%
And so therefore,
advertising is monotone

00:36:26.140 --> 00:36:29.308 align:middle line:90%
decreasing in the market size.

00:36:29.308 --> 00:36:30.850 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, this
is what you would

00:36:30.850 --> 00:36:33.490 align:middle line:84%
do if you knew entry was
never going to occur,

00:36:33.490 --> 00:36:35.865 align:middle line:84%
and this is what you would do
if you knew entry was going

00:36:35.865 --> 00:36:38.420 align:middle line:90%
to occur with probability 1.

00:36:38.420 --> 00:36:41.030 align:middle line:84%
But if you look at the
strategic entry in this model,

00:36:41.030 --> 00:36:42.620 align:middle line:84%
the strategic entry
will be like this.

00:36:42.620 --> 00:36:45.140 align:middle line:84%
It'll have this
non-monotone pattern where

00:36:45.140 --> 00:36:48.800 align:middle line:84%
it departs from the
strategic one, dips way down,

00:36:48.800 --> 00:36:50.420 align:middle line:90%
and then comes back up.

00:36:50.420 --> 00:36:52.130 align:middle line:90%
What's this dip?

00:36:52.130 --> 00:36:56.690 align:middle line:84%
The dip is I'm trying to
keep my market small enough

00:36:56.690 --> 00:36:59.520 align:middle line:84%
so that the generics don't
come in and compete with me.

00:36:59.520 --> 00:37:02.030 align:middle line:84%
So what I do is I
do less advertising.

00:37:02.030 --> 00:37:04.910 align:middle line:84%
With less advertising,
I have lower sales.

00:37:04.910 --> 00:37:07.922 align:middle line:84%
Lower sales, less likely
there's a generic competitor.

00:37:07.922 --> 00:37:09.380 align:middle line:84%
And so what I'm
doing in some sense

00:37:09.380 --> 00:37:11.870 align:middle line:84%
is I'm keeping the
market small so

00:37:11.870 --> 00:37:14.540 align:middle line:84%
that I can get monopoly
profits in the small market

00:37:14.540 --> 00:37:18.370 align:middle line:84%
instead of getting oligopoly
profits in a bigger market.

00:37:18.370 --> 00:37:21.660 align:middle line:90%


00:37:21.660 --> 00:37:23.140 align:middle line:90%
And at some point, I give up.

00:37:23.140 --> 00:37:25.902 align:middle line:84%
Here, I start increasing
my advertising again

00:37:25.902 --> 00:37:27.610 align:middle line:84%
because I realize that
generics are going

00:37:27.610 --> 00:37:28.693 align:middle line:90%
to come in no matter what.

00:37:28.693 --> 00:37:31.600 align:middle line:84%
So I might as well be splitting
the best possible market

00:37:31.600 --> 00:37:33.670 align:middle line:84%
with the generic
competitor rather than

00:37:33.670 --> 00:37:36.340 align:middle line:84%
trying to keep it small enough
to keep the generics out.

00:37:36.340 --> 00:37:40.930 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so the example above,
I was comparing investment

00:37:40.930 --> 00:37:42.500 align:middle line:90%
in two different models.

00:37:42.500 --> 00:37:45.490 align:middle line:84%
The upper line that
I drew for you,

00:37:45.490 --> 00:37:49.330 align:middle line:84%
that was the line for this no
entry deterrence motive model

00:37:49.330 --> 00:37:52.780 align:middle line:84%
where the potential entrant only
observes the advertising level

00:37:52.780 --> 00:37:56.080 align:middle line:84%
A after it's come into
the market or not.

00:37:56.080 --> 00:37:59.860 align:middle line:84%
And then the other line was
the standard strategic entry

00:37:59.860 --> 00:38:03.145 align:middle line:84%
deterrence model where I
choose A, they observe A,

00:38:03.145 --> 00:38:04.780 align:middle line:90%
and they can stay out.

00:38:04.780 --> 00:38:10.803 align:middle line:84%
And so our argument was that
the relationship between market

00:38:10.803 --> 00:38:11.303 align:middle line:90%
size--

00:38:11.303 --> 00:38:15.240 align:middle line:90%


00:38:15.240 --> 00:38:17.220 align:middle line:84%
determining market size
in the investment, A,

00:38:17.220 --> 00:38:22.890 align:middle line:84%
was going to be monotone on the
bottom model and non-monotone

00:38:22.890 --> 00:38:26.280 align:middle line:90%
in the model on the top.

00:38:26.280 --> 00:38:29.110 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, the paper
tries to explain

00:38:29.110 --> 00:38:33.880 align:middle line:84%
why is it that we're going to
see these monotone patterns

00:38:33.880 --> 00:38:36.690 align:middle line:90%
versus non-monotone patterns?

00:38:36.690 --> 00:38:39.240 align:middle line:84%
How do we think about-- the
strategic entry deterrence

00:38:39.240 --> 00:38:43.790 align:middle line:84%
model, you have a profit
function pi 1 of A.

00:38:43.790 --> 00:38:48.320 align:middle line:84%
And I guess I didn't
say here, you know,

00:38:48.320 --> 00:38:51.710 align:middle line:84%
in all of Tirole's
models, Tirole always

00:38:51.710 --> 00:38:54.710 align:middle line:84%
has the entry cost,
E being known.

00:38:54.710 --> 00:38:56.570 align:middle line:84%
And the entry cost,
E, being known

00:38:56.570 --> 00:38:59.030 align:middle line:84%
means if I want to
keep the entrant out,

00:38:59.030 --> 00:39:00.450 align:middle line:90%
I have to distort this far.

00:39:00.450 --> 00:39:02.867 align:middle line:84%
And going this far keeps them
out, or going less than that

00:39:02.867 --> 00:39:03.960 align:middle line:90%
doesn't keep them out.

00:39:03.960 --> 00:39:06.170 align:middle line:84%
In empirical work,
we're always going

00:39:06.170 --> 00:39:08.600 align:middle line:84%
to be making the
entry cost, E, random

00:39:08.600 --> 00:39:13.820 align:middle line:84%
and have it unpredictable to the
incumbent exactly what's going

00:39:13.820 --> 00:39:17.020 align:middle line:90%
to keep the entrant out or not.

00:39:17.020 --> 00:39:23.570 align:middle line:84%
So if I think about what's my
profit if I choose investment A,

00:39:23.570 --> 00:39:25.070 align:middle line:90%
I don't know the entry cost.

00:39:25.070 --> 00:39:29.630 align:middle line:84%
So assume that the entry cost,
E, has some distribution, F.

00:39:29.630 --> 00:39:35.270 align:middle line:84%
So F of pi 2 duopoly of A is
the probability that my rival's

00:39:35.270 --> 00:39:38.600 align:middle line:84%
entry cost draw is low
enough that the rival comes

00:39:38.600 --> 00:39:41.120 align:middle line:84%
in and competes with
me, in which case

00:39:41.120 --> 00:39:47.080 align:middle line:84%
I will get the duopoly profits
that you get after investment A.

00:39:47.080 --> 00:39:50.420 align:middle line:84%
The other case is when
the entry cost is high.

00:39:50.420 --> 00:39:53.950 align:middle line:84%
If the entry cost is high, which
happens with 1 minus F of pi 2D

00:39:53.950 --> 00:39:58.030 align:middle line:84%
star of A, then I'm going to get
to be a monopolist and get this

00:39:58.030 --> 00:39:59.380 align:middle line:90%
profit level.

00:39:59.380 --> 00:40:02.210 align:middle line:84%
And then I have a cost
of doing the advertising.

00:40:02.210 --> 00:40:06.910 align:middle line:90%


00:40:06.910 --> 00:40:11.330 align:middle line:84%
The model on the
bottom of my board

00:40:11.330 --> 00:40:13.250 align:middle line:84%
was different in
that the entrant only

00:40:13.250 --> 00:40:16.370 align:middle line:84%
observed A after making
its entry decision.

00:40:16.370 --> 00:40:18.290 align:middle line:84%
So again, this is a
shorthand for your not

00:40:18.290 --> 00:40:22.460 align:middle line:84%
thinking about the effect
of A on the entrant.

00:40:22.460 --> 00:40:27.740 align:middle line:84%
So if the entrant
wasn't observing A,

00:40:27.740 --> 00:40:29.750 align:middle line:90%
what advertising would you do?

00:40:29.750 --> 00:40:32.000 align:middle line:84%
There, the entry
decision doesn't

00:40:32.000 --> 00:40:35.700 align:middle line:84%
depend on A. The entry
deterrent depends on A star--

00:40:35.700 --> 00:40:40.500 align:middle line:84%
what the incumbent
expects you to-- what

00:40:40.500 --> 00:40:42.783 align:middle line:84%
the potential entrant
expects you to invest.

00:40:42.783 --> 00:40:44.700 align:middle line:84%
So you want to think
about writing your profit

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:46.290 align:middle line:90%
as a function of two things--

00:40:46.290 --> 00:40:48.870 align:middle line:84%
the actual advertising
that you do,

00:40:48.870 --> 00:40:51.166 align:middle line:84%
and the equilibrium
level of advertising.

00:40:51.166 --> 00:40:53.984 align:middle line:90%


00:40:53.984 --> 00:40:59.120 align:middle line:84%
And here, the firm
enters if its entry cost

00:40:59.120 --> 00:41:00.980 align:middle line:84%
is less than what
duopoly profits would

00:41:00.980 --> 00:41:04.040 align:middle line:84%
be at the equilibrium
level of advertising.

00:41:04.040 --> 00:41:06.020 align:middle line:84%
But then, the actual
profits received

00:41:06.020 --> 00:41:08.490 align:middle line:84%
depend on the true
level of advertising.

00:41:08.490 --> 00:41:10.790 align:middle line:84%
Again, entry depends on
the equilibrium level

00:41:10.790 --> 00:41:12.180 align:middle line:90%
of advertising.

00:41:12.180 --> 00:41:15.270 align:middle line:84%
Monopoly profit depends
on the true advertising.

00:41:15.270 --> 00:41:17.960 align:middle line:84%
Cost of it depends on
the true advertising.

00:41:17.960 --> 00:41:21.470 align:middle line:84%
So when I think about the first
order conditions for this profit

00:41:21.470 --> 00:41:23.600 align:middle line:84%
maximization problem
versus this one,

00:41:23.600 --> 00:41:25.250 align:middle line:84%
the two first order
conditions are

00:41:25.250 --> 00:41:28.130 align:middle line:84%
going to differ
because of the fact

00:41:28.130 --> 00:41:31.800 align:middle line:84%
that here, there's a derivative
with respect to A in this term,

00:41:31.800 --> 00:41:35.120 align:middle line:84%
and here, changing A
doesn't affect this term.

00:41:35.120 --> 00:41:38.160 align:middle line:84%
So that's what makes the
two problems different.

00:41:38.160 --> 00:41:40.750 align:middle line:90%


00:41:40.750 --> 00:41:43.010 align:middle line:90%
What is the extra term?

00:41:43.010 --> 00:41:45.610 align:middle line:84%
So they have the same
first order conditions.

00:41:45.610 --> 00:41:48.340 align:middle line:84%
Except if you think about
what's the effect that

00:41:48.340 --> 00:41:50.710 align:middle line:84%
comes from differentiating
this with respect to A,

00:41:50.710 --> 00:41:55.060 align:middle line:84%
it's that it's the derivative
of this with respect to A times

00:41:55.060 --> 00:42:00.340 align:middle line:84%
this plus the derivative of this
with respect to A times that.

00:42:00.340 --> 00:42:02.030 align:middle line:90%
That's the new term.

00:42:02.030 --> 00:42:05.830 align:middle line:84%
So the extra entry
deterrence term is--

00:42:05.830 --> 00:42:09.360 align:middle line:90%
and this is intuitive--

00:42:09.360 --> 00:42:14.850 align:middle line:84%
how much does A affect
the duopoly profits

00:42:14.850 --> 00:42:20.980 align:middle line:84%
that the firm is going to
get times the probability

00:42:20.980 --> 00:42:24.130 align:middle line:84%
that that effect
on profits is going

00:42:24.130 --> 00:42:26.560 align:middle line:90%
to affect 2's entry decision.

00:42:26.560 --> 00:42:28.990 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to affect 2's
entry decision if and only

00:42:28.990 --> 00:42:33.610 align:middle line:84%
if his entry cost is
identical to the profits,

00:42:33.610 --> 00:42:36.410 align:middle line:90%
or within D pi of the profits.

00:42:36.410 --> 00:42:42.617 align:middle line:84%
And then if you do effect entry,
the extra profit that you get

00:42:42.617 --> 00:42:44.200 align:middle line:84%
is the difference
between the monopoly

00:42:44.200 --> 00:42:45.660 align:middle line:90%
profit and the duopoly profit.

00:42:45.660 --> 00:42:49.004 align:middle line:90%


00:42:49.004 --> 00:42:50.600 align:middle line:90%
And this term--

00:42:50.600 --> 00:42:52.570 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE] says that?

00:42:52.570 --> 00:42:53.070 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:42:53.070 --> 00:42:55.360 align:middle line:90%
OK, yeah.

00:42:55.360 --> 00:42:59.770 align:middle line:84%
This term is where the
non-monotonicity comes from,

00:42:59.770 --> 00:43:03.190 align:middle line:84%
because this term is
multiplied by the likelihood

00:43:03.190 --> 00:43:05.620 align:middle line:84%
that a small decrease
in an entrant's profits

00:43:05.620 --> 00:43:08.900 align:middle line:84%
is going to be pivotal
to their entry decision.

00:43:08.900 --> 00:43:11.092 align:middle line:84%
And so this is what creates
the non-monotonicity.

00:43:11.092 --> 00:43:13.550 align:middle line:84%
You know, this we think of as
a big number between monopoly

00:43:13.550 --> 00:43:15.120 align:middle line:90%
and duopoly profits.

00:43:15.120 --> 00:43:17.690 align:middle line:84%
This is just whatever
effect your advertising

00:43:17.690 --> 00:43:18.663 align:middle line:90%
has on the rival.

00:43:18.663 --> 00:43:20.330 align:middle line:84%
But the thing that
makes it non-monotone

00:43:20.330 --> 00:43:23.130 align:middle line:84%
is, is that change in
your rival's profit

00:43:23.130 --> 00:43:25.310 align:middle line:84%
is going to be pivotal to
whether they enter or not?

00:43:25.310 --> 00:43:27.180 align:middle line:84%
And that's going
to be non-monotone.

00:43:27.180 --> 00:43:31.836 align:middle line:90%


00:43:31.836 --> 00:43:35.740 align:middle line:84%
And then the second effect, I
said this earlier in the paper,

00:43:35.740 --> 00:43:39.910 align:middle line:84%
there are these two reasons
why investment normally

00:43:39.910 --> 00:43:41.770 align:middle line:90%
changes in market size.

00:43:41.770 --> 00:43:46.430 align:middle line:84%
It changes for this direct
effect which I define here.

00:43:46.430 --> 00:43:50.650 align:middle line:84%
So there's this direct
effect, which is just

00:43:50.650 --> 00:43:52.640 align:middle line:90%
its direct effect on pi 2.

00:43:52.640 --> 00:43:56.500 align:middle line:84%
And then there's the
competition effect.

00:43:56.500 --> 00:43:58.390 align:middle line:84%
So the competition
effect is, do you

00:43:58.390 --> 00:44:06.520 align:middle line:84%
want to do more in a
duopoly or a monopoly?

00:44:06.520 --> 00:44:10.600 align:middle line:84%
And the direct effect
is, do you want

00:44:10.600 --> 00:44:14.050 align:middle line:84%
to do more in a big
market, or a small market?

00:44:14.050 --> 00:44:15.940 align:middle line:84%
And so in some
sense, the theorem

00:44:15.940 --> 00:44:18.760 align:middle line:84%
says that there are, in that
model, exactly two reasons

00:44:18.760 --> 00:44:22.630 align:middle line:84%
why you're changing investment
as market size increases.

00:44:22.630 --> 00:44:24.130 align:middle line:84%
One is because of
the direct effect,

00:44:24.130 --> 00:44:26.130 align:middle line:84%
and the other is because
the competition effect.

00:44:26.130 --> 00:44:28.870 align:middle line:84%
If those two go in
the same direction,

00:44:28.870 --> 00:44:33.415 align:middle line:84%
then the investment
would be monotone.

00:44:33.415 --> 00:44:35.540 align:middle line:84%
And we can think about
whether we expect investment

00:44:35.540 --> 00:44:37.290 align:middle line:84%
to be monotone by just
thinking about what

00:44:37.290 --> 00:44:41.300 align:middle line:84%
are direct and competition
effects of an investment.

00:44:41.300 --> 00:44:46.460 align:middle line:84%
And what I'd done in
this model is clearly,

00:44:46.460 --> 00:44:50.300 align:middle line:84%
the fact that the advertising
raised the value for the generic

00:44:50.300 --> 00:44:52.800 align:middle line:84%
is what gave it a negative
competition effect.

00:44:52.800 --> 00:44:54.800 align:middle line:84%
If my rival is going to
be benefiting from that

00:44:54.800 --> 00:44:57.200 align:middle line:90%
and raising--

00:44:57.200 --> 00:45:03.320 align:middle line:84%
I'm getting a smaller
value in a duopoly,

00:45:03.320 --> 00:45:06.530 align:middle line:84%
I eliminated the
direct effect by saying

00:45:06.530 --> 00:45:10.650 align:middle line:84%
A is a per-consumer
spending on advertising.

00:45:10.650 --> 00:45:12.110 align:middle line:84%
And so it's a
per-consumer spending

00:45:12.110 --> 00:45:14.420 align:middle line:84%
for a per-consumer
benefit, so there's

00:45:14.420 --> 00:45:16.790 align:middle line:84%
no direct effect,
big versus small,

00:45:16.790 --> 00:45:19.670 align:middle line:84%
if you're thinking of this
as per-consumer spending.

00:45:19.670 --> 00:45:22.190 align:middle line:84%
And that technology where
you're visiting doctors,

00:45:22.190 --> 00:45:24.260 align:middle line:84%
and the number of
doctors you have to visit

00:45:24.260 --> 00:45:26.060 align:middle line:84%
is proportional
number of patients,

00:45:26.060 --> 00:45:29.060 align:middle line:84%
it's plausible that
the per-consumer cost

00:45:29.060 --> 00:45:31.100 align:middle line:84%
and the per-consumer
benefit are the same.

00:45:31.100 --> 00:45:34.320 align:middle line:84%
So I've just basically
eliminated direct effect.

00:45:34.320 --> 00:45:36.050 align:middle line:84%
And so once you
eliminate direct effects,

00:45:36.050 --> 00:45:37.550 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be
monotone increasing

00:45:37.550 --> 00:45:39.380 align:middle line:84%
or monotone decreasing depending
on whether the competition

00:45:39.380 --> 00:45:41.280 align:middle line:90%
effect is positive or negative.

00:45:41.280 --> 00:45:44.300 align:middle line:90%


00:45:44.300 --> 00:45:45.870 align:middle line:90%
Anyway, paper does other stuff.

00:45:45.870 --> 00:45:51.890 align:middle line:84%
So let me go to empirical
application here.

00:45:51.890 --> 00:45:55.140 align:middle line:84%
There are 63 branded
drugs in our data sample.

00:45:55.140 --> 00:45:58.340 align:middle line:84%
All the branded drugs
lost patent protection

00:45:58.340 --> 00:46:02.060 align:middle line:84%
at some point between
1986 and 1992.

00:46:02.060 --> 00:46:05.720 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going
to think about

00:46:05.720 --> 00:46:07.610 align:middle line:84%
how does the behavior
of incumbents

00:46:07.610 --> 00:46:09.663 align:middle line:84%
compare between big
and small markets?

00:46:09.663 --> 00:46:14.000 align:middle line:90%


00:46:14.000 --> 00:46:15.950 align:middle line:90%
So we're going to put--

00:46:15.950 --> 00:46:17.960 align:middle line:84%
generally, you're
going to make up

00:46:17.960 --> 00:46:22.250 align:middle line:84%
graphs where we have
market size on the x-axis.

00:46:22.250 --> 00:46:29.310 align:middle line:84%
What market size means for us
is revenue some number of years

00:46:29.310 --> 00:46:30.655 align:middle line:90%
prior to patent expiration.

00:46:30.655 --> 00:46:33.030 align:middle line:84%
The thought is that if you're
going to be doing something

00:46:33.030 --> 00:46:35.370 align:middle line:84%
with your drug to
deter entry, that

00:46:35.370 --> 00:46:37.155 align:middle line:90%
comes closer to the entry date.

00:46:37.155 --> 00:46:39.780 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going to look a few
years back from the entry date

00:46:39.780 --> 00:46:41.520 align:middle line:90%
and say, how big was the market?

00:46:41.520 --> 00:46:45.330 align:middle line:84%
That's our possibly exogenous
measure of market size.

00:46:45.330 --> 00:46:48.750 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to look
at for potential strategic

00:46:48.750 --> 00:46:50.490 align:middle line:90%
investments.

00:46:50.490 --> 00:46:52.540 align:middle line:90%
One is detail advertising.

00:46:52.540 --> 00:46:54.720 align:middle line:90%
So detail advertising is--

00:46:54.720 --> 00:46:56.730 align:middle line:84%
strangely enough,
data on this exists--

00:46:56.730 --> 00:46:59.250 align:middle line:84%
the expenditures by
pharmaceutical firms

00:46:59.250 --> 00:47:02.010 align:middle line:84%
sending sales representatives
to doctors offices

00:47:02.010 --> 00:47:03.880 align:middle line:90%
to tell them about their drugs.

00:47:03.880 --> 00:47:06.515 align:middle line:84%
And we have data on
that at the drug level.

00:47:06.515 --> 00:47:09.330 align:middle line:90%


00:47:09.330 --> 00:47:14.920 align:middle line:84%
Second strategic investment we
have is journal advertising.

00:47:14.920 --> 00:47:18.840 align:middle line:84%
This is-- one thing you do
with drugs is advertise them

00:47:18.840 --> 00:47:21.550 align:middle line:84%
in medical journals that
are sold to doctors.

00:47:21.550 --> 00:47:24.240 align:middle line:84%
And so we know, for
each drug in our sample,

00:47:24.240 --> 00:47:27.300 align:middle line:84%
how much money they were
spending advertising them

00:47:27.300 --> 00:47:30.400 align:middle line:90%
to physicians.

00:47:30.400 --> 00:47:33.070 align:middle line:84%
Third strategic
investment we look at

00:47:33.070 --> 00:47:35.920 align:middle line:90%
is presentation proliferation.

00:47:35.920 --> 00:47:38.410 align:middle line:84%
Something that
people have discussed

00:47:38.410 --> 00:47:42.400 align:middle line:84%
firms doing to deter
entry is, if you

00:47:42.400 --> 00:47:46.550 align:middle line:84%
go into your doctor with
a prescription for--

00:47:46.550 --> 00:47:48.550 align:middle line:84%
you go into the pharmacy
and have a prescription

00:47:48.550 --> 00:47:54.250 align:middle line:84%
for a 50-milligram
tablet of some medicine,

00:47:54.250 --> 00:47:56.960 align:middle line:84%
and then they don't have
a 50 milligram tablet,

00:47:56.960 --> 00:47:59.530 align:middle line:84%
they have a 100
milligram tablet.

00:47:59.530 --> 00:48:02.255 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, they can give you
the 100 milligram tablets,

00:48:02.255 --> 00:48:03.880 align:middle line:84%
and give you a little
pill cutter thing

00:48:03.880 --> 00:48:05.150 align:middle line:90%
that cuts the pills in half.

00:48:05.150 --> 00:48:06.940 align:middle line:84%
And you can turn
one pill into two,

00:48:06.940 --> 00:48:09.850 align:middle line:84%
and take half of the
100 milligram pill,

00:48:09.850 --> 00:48:11.570 align:middle line:90%
but sometimes you can't.

00:48:11.570 --> 00:48:13.810 align:middle line:84%
And certainly, if
they've given you

00:48:13.810 --> 00:48:17.415 align:middle line:84%
a prescription for an ointment
that contains this drug,

00:48:17.415 --> 00:48:18.790 align:middle line:84%
and they don't
have the ointment,

00:48:18.790 --> 00:48:20.680 align:middle line:84%
they only have the
oral, the pharmacist

00:48:20.680 --> 00:48:23.728 align:middle line:84%
can't sell you the generic
ointment when you're

00:48:23.728 --> 00:48:25.020 align:middle line:90%
supposed to be taking the pill.

00:48:25.020 --> 00:48:26.250 align:middle line:84%
They can't sell you
the gel cap if you're

00:48:26.250 --> 00:48:27.840 align:middle line:84%
supposed to be taking
the pill, or the liquid

00:48:27.840 --> 00:48:29.740 align:middle line:84%
if you're supposed to be
taking the pill, or whatever.

00:48:29.740 --> 00:48:32.282 align:middle line:84%
And so something you've heard
that pharmaceutical firms might

00:48:32.282 --> 00:48:35.610 align:middle line:84%
do to deter entry
is start producing

00:48:35.610 --> 00:48:38.940 align:middle line:84%
the drug in a zillion different
varieties to, in some sense,

00:48:38.940 --> 00:48:42.760 align:middle line:84%
raise the cost of the entrance
to come in and compete with you.

00:48:42.760 --> 00:48:45.660 align:middle line:84%
So if, instead of having
one standard pill,

00:48:45.660 --> 00:48:46.590 align:middle line:90%
I have a whole bunch--

00:48:46.590 --> 00:48:48.548 align:middle line:84%
I have a regular pill,
and a time release pill,

00:48:48.548 --> 00:48:50.340 align:middle line:84%
and a gel cap, and a
something or other--

00:48:50.340 --> 00:48:53.565 align:middle line:90%
I can have many different--

00:48:53.565 --> 00:48:55.440 align:middle line:84%
if the generic only came
in and only produced

00:48:55.440 --> 00:48:57.610 align:middle line:84%
the most popular
presentation of the drug,

00:48:57.610 --> 00:49:00.027 align:middle line:84%
it would only be competing for
a small part of the market.

00:49:00.027 --> 00:49:02.520 align:middle line:84%
And that could make it
more difficult to enter.

00:49:02.520 --> 00:49:06.280 align:middle line:84%
And then the final thing
that we do is just prices.

00:49:06.280 --> 00:49:11.870 align:middle line:84%
And again, I discussed
some of these issues

00:49:11.870 --> 00:49:14.870 align:middle line:84%
about the entry deterrence--
the entry deterrence

00:49:14.870 --> 00:49:17.660 align:middle line:84%
through signaling costs,
or signaling something

00:49:17.660 --> 00:49:20.120 align:middle line:84%
about demand, or something
where you set low prices

00:49:20.120 --> 00:49:21.990 align:middle line:90%
to try to deter entry.

00:49:21.990 --> 00:49:25.040 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going to
look at these investments

00:49:25.040 --> 00:49:28.850 align:middle line:84%
and how the investments
look when you graph them

00:49:28.850 --> 00:49:29.837 align:middle line:90%
against market size.

00:49:29.837 --> 00:49:30.920 align:middle line:90%
And do they look monotone?

00:49:30.920 --> 00:49:32.520 align:middle line:90%
Do they look non-monotone?

00:49:32.520 --> 00:49:34.250 align:middle line:84%
And what we're going
to particularly do

00:49:34.250 --> 00:49:38.930 align:middle line:84%
is focus on four drugs that
are in that intermediate range

00:49:38.930 --> 00:49:40.910 align:middle line:90%
where entry is uncertain.

00:49:40.910 --> 00:49:43.100 align:middle line:84%
Is that where we see a
distortion in the direction

00:49:43.100 --> 00:49:46.430 align:middle line:84%
that you would expect
the distortion?

00:49:46.430 --> 00:49:49.280 align:middle line:84%
And what distortions
would you expect?

00:49:49.280 --> 00:49:51.200 align:middle line:84%
If I'm trying to
deter entry, I'm

00:49:51.200 --> 00:49:53.480 align:middle line:84%
going to do less detail
advertising because I'm trying

00:49:53.480 --> 00:49:55.130 align:middle line:90%
to keep the market small.

00:49:55.130 --> 00:49:57.710 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to do less journal
advertising because I'm trying

00:49:57.710 --> 00:49:59.310 align:middle line:90%
to keep the market small.

00:49:59.310 --> 00:50:01.790 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to do more
presentation proliferation

00:50:01.790 --> 00:50:04.910 align:middle line:84%
and introduce more
versions of my drug,

00:50:04.910 --> 00:50:07.760 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going to
set lower prices.

00:50:07.760 --> 00:50:09.990 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to
look for are they

00:50:09.990 --> 00:50:13.140 align:middle line:84%
non-monotone with those
deviations from monotonicity

00:50:13.140 --> 00:50:15.060 align:middle line:84%
in the range where
it's plausible

00:50:15.060 --> 00:50:18.750 align:middle line:90%
that you could keep rivals out?

00:50:18.750 --> 00:50:21.580 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to look at
this in two different ways.

00:50:21.580 --> 00:50:24.330 align:middle line:84%
One, just in the
pure cross section,

00:50:24.330 --> 00:50:27.180 align:middle line:84%
advertising versus market
size, is advertising

00:50:27.180 --> 00:50:29.280 align:middle line:90%
non-monotone in revenue?

00:50:29.280 --> 00:50:32.353 align:middle line:84%
But sometimes, these things
are just super steeply

00:50:32.353 --> 00:50:34.020 align:middle line:84%
increasing depending
on how you describe

00:50:34.020 --> 00:50:36.187 align:middle line:84%
the variables, because just
bigger markets-- like TV

00:50:36.187 --> 00:50:37.710 align:middle line:84%
advertising, this
would never work

00:50:37.710 --> 00:50:39.990 align:middle line:84%
because TV advertising,
the bigger the market is,

00:50:39.990 --> 00:50:42.090 align:middle line:90%
the more you spend.

00:50:42.090 --> 00:50:44.760 align:middle line:84%
So the other thing
we do is look at how

00:50:44.760 --> 00:50:47.588 align:middle line:84%
are firms changing
advertising in the year

00:50:47.588 --> 00:50:49.380 align:middle line:84%
the patent's expiring
relative to what they

00:50:49.380 --> 00:50:51.980 align:middle line:84%
were doing two years
before the patent expires?

00:50:51.980 --> 00:50:54.780 align:middle line:84%
And so you can think of it as
it's like we're using AT minus 2

00:50:54.780 --> 00:50:57.510 align:middle line:84%
as the control level
of the investment,

00:50:57.510 --> 00:51:00.360 align:middle line:84%
and think about how are
they changing investments?

00:51:00.360 --> 00:51:05.895 align:middle line:84%
And is the change in investment
non-monotone in that kind

00:51:05.895 --> 00:51:06.395 align:middle line:90%
of pattern?

00:51:06.395 --> 00:51:10.570 align:middle line:90%


00:51:10.570 --> 00:51:12.420 align:middle line:84%
And what are some
of the findings?

00:51:12.420 --> 00:51:15.940 align:middle line:84%
And again, we're very
conservative in this paper

00:51:15.940 --> 00:51:19.450 align:middle line:84%
in looking for general
tests of non-monotonicity,

00:51:19.450 --> 00:51:21.520 align:middle line:84%
non-parametrically,
rather than just putting

00:51:21.520 --> 00:51:22.910 align:middle line:90%
in quadratic functions.

00:51:22.910 --> 00:51:25.160 align:middle line:84%
And so nothing's all
that significant.

00:51:25.160 --> 00:51:27.610 align:middle line:84%
But there are a few places
where it looks like we

00:51:27.610 --> 00:51:29.350 align:middle line:90%
find these non-monotonicities.

00:51:29.350 --> 00:51:33.770 align:middle line:84%
The first is in the detail
in journal advertising.

00:51:33.770 --> 00:51:38.950 align:middle line:84%
When you just graph them
versus the market size,

00:51:38.950 --> 00:51:41.440 align:middle line:84%
it looks like the
detail advertising--

00:51:41.440 --> 00:51:42.770 align:middle line:90%
these are just the means--

00:51:42.770 --> 00:51:44.830 align:middle line:84%
the detail advertising
is relatively

00:51:44.830 --> 00:51:47.290 align:middle line:84%
low for the second
quintile drugs

00:51:47.290 --> 00:51:49.630 align:middle line:90%
relative to the other drugs.

00:51:49.630 --> 00:51:52.480 align:middle line:84%
And the journal
advertising is relatively

00:51:52.480 --> 00:51:54.610 align:middle line:84%
low for the second
quintile drugs

00:51:54.610 --> 00:51:56.720 align:middle line:90%
relative to the other drugs.

00:51:56.720 --> 00:51:59.500 align:middle line:84%
So it looks like there's
this cross-sectional pattern

00:51:59.500 --> 00:52:01.540 align:middle line:84%
in the detail in
journal advertising

00:52:01.540 --> 00:52:03.820 align:middle line:84%
where in these second
quintile drugs where

00:52:03.820 --> 00:52:07.616 align:middle line:84%
the entry is uncertain, there
is lower levels of advertising.

00:52:07.616 --> 00:52:10.600 align:middle line:90%


00:52:10.600 --> 00:52:16.480 align:middle line:84%
But marginally significant once
you test it non-parametrically.

00:52:16.480 --> 00:52:23.290 align:middle line:84%
And then the other
thing we look for is

00:52:23.290 --> 00:52:28.000 align:middle line:84%
how are things changing as
patent expiration approaches?

00:52:28.000 --> 00:52:32.620 align:middle line:84%
And here, we get some
marginally significant results

00:52:32.620 --> 00:52:35.080 align:middle line:84%
on the presentation
proliferation

00:52:35.080 --> 00:52:38.200 align:middle line:90%
and on the journal advertising.

00:52:38.200 --> 00:52:41.590 align:middle line:84%
The journal advertising is
going in the wrong direction,

00:52:41.590 --> 00:52:43.660 align:middle line:84%
but the presentation
Herfindahl, there's

00:52:43.660 --> 00:52:46.900 align:middle line:84%
some evidence of the
presentation proliferation

00:52:46.900 --> 00:52:50.182 align:middle line:84%
also going up for these
second quintile drugs.

00:52:50.182 --> 00:52:51.640 align:middle line:84%
This looks like
they're introducing

00:52:51.640 --> 00:52:54.070 align:middle line:84%
new versions of the
second quintile drugs

00:52:54.070 --> 00:52:56.890 align:middle line:84%
as the patent is expiring that
they hadn't introduced years

00:52:56.890 --> 00:52:58.430 align:middle line:84%
before when they
were monopolist.

00:52:58.430 --> 00:53:03.860 align:middle line:90%


00:53:03.860 --> 00:53:06.010 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

00:53:06.010 --> 00:53:06.680 align:middle line:90%
Comments?

00:53:06.680 --> 00:53:08.840 align:middle line:90%
No?

00:53:08.840 --> 00:53:10.980 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:53:10.980 --> 00:53:15.650 align:middle line:84%
So the third paper, this one
I'm going to do super quickly.

00:53:15.650 --> 00:53:22.250 align:middle line:84%
Goolsbee-Syverson is-- it's
another reduced form prediction

00:53:22.250 --> 00:53:24.680 align:middle line:84%
paper looking at
some prediction that

00:53:24.680 --> 00:53:29.000 align:middle line:84%
would hold for strategic models
versus non-strategic models.

00:53:29.000 --> 00:53:34.790 align:middle line:84%
The prediction that they explore
is, how do firms change prices

00:53:34.790 --> 00:53:38.010 align:middle line:84%
when they become threatened
by Southwest's entry

00:53:38.010 --> 00:53:42.236 align:middle line:84%
even though Southwest
has not entered?

00:53:42.236 --> 00:53:45.860 align:middle line:84%
And so what's really nice
about this application is,

00:53:45.860 --> 00:53:49.670 align:middle line:90%
imagine you have a market--

00:53:49.670 --> 00:53:52.550 align:middle line:84%
so we have a market that
Southwest is not currently

00:53:52.550 --> 00:54:00.350 align:middle line:84%
serving, like flying
from Boston to Nashville.

00:54:00.350 --> 00:54:03.990 align:middle line:84%
And there are no
flights on Southwest

00:54:03.990 --> 00:54:05.670 align:middle line:90%
from Boston to Nashville.

00:54:05.670 --> 00:54:09.720 align:middle line:84%
And the current carriers
serving Boston to Nashville

00:54:09.720 --> 00:54:13.680 align:middle line:84%
are completely unscared of
Southwest entering and competing

00:54:13.680 --> 00:54:15.690 align:middle line:84%
with them because
Southwest doesn't have

00:54:15.690 --> 00:54:16.968 align:middle line:90%
any operations in Nashville.

00:54:16.968 --> 00:54:18.010 align:middle line:90%
They have no gates there.

00:54:18.010 --> 00:54:19.177 align:middle line:90%
They have no aircraft there.

00:54:19.177 --> 00:54:20.530 align:middle line:90%
They have no employees there.

00:54:20.530 --> 00:54:22.982 align:middle line:84%
If Southwest doesn't have
any employees in Nashville,

00:54:22.982 --> 00:54:24.690 align:middle line:84%
it's unlikely they're
going to just start

00:54:24.690 --> 00:54:27.534 align:middle line:90%
a Boston to Nashville flight.

00:54:27.534 --> 00:54:32.680 align:middle line:84%
But suppose Southwest does
operate out of Boston.

00:54:32.680 --> 00:54:34.710 align:middle line:84%
Suppose Southwest
were to announce,

00:54:34.710 --> 00:54:36.870 align:middle line:84%
we're moving into Nashville,
and we're initially

00:54:36.870 --> 00:54:40.710 align:middle line:84%
going to fly Nashville to Miami,
Nashville to Dallas, Nashville

00:54:40.710 --> 00:54:45.390 align:middle line:90%
to Las Vegas, Nashville to LA.

00:54:45.390 --> 00:54:48.390 align:middle line:84%
Well, then, if you're the
carriers on the Boston

00:54:48.390 --> 00:54:51.930 align:middle line:84%
to Nashville route,
you become scared

00:54:51.930 --> 00:54:56.640 align:middle line:84%
that Southwest, now that they
have operations in both Boston

00:54:56.640 --> 00:54:59.040 align:middle line:84%
and Nashville, are they
going to start serving

00:54:59.040 --> 00:55:01.200 align:middle line:90%
the Boston to Nashville market?

00:55:01.200 --> 00:55:04.950 align:middle line:84%
But the nice feature that
Goolsbee and Syverson note

00:55:04.950 --> 00:55:09.030 align:middle line:84%
is that Southwest both
announces that they're

00:55:09.030 --> 00:55:12.810 align:middle line:84%
coming to Nashville five months
before they come to Nashville,

00:55:12.810 --> 00:55:14.610 align:middle line:84%
and when they
announce we're going

00:55:14.610 --> 00:55:16.420 align:middle line:84%
to come to Nashville
with these routes,

00:55:16.420 --> 00:55:19.440 align:middle line:84%
so you know they're not
serving Boston to Nashville.

00:55:19.440 --> 00:55:22.320 align:middle line:84%
And so what Goolsbee
and Syverson say is,

00:55:22.320 --> 00:55:25.740 align:middle line:84%
let's imagine that firms
are trying to keep--

00:55:25.740 --> 00:55:31.410 align:middle line:84%
that once this happens,
suddenly United Airlines

00:55:31.410 --> 00:55:35.820 align:middle line:84%
has an incentive to try
to keep Southwest out

00:55:35.820 --> 00:55:37.210 align:middle line:90%
of the Boston Nashville market.

00:55:37.210 --> 00:55:38.390 align:middle line:90%
What might they do?

00:55:38.390 --> 00:55:41.460 align:middle line:84%
They're looking at, again, this
limit pricing thought of they

00:55:41.460 --> 00:55:45.940 align:middle line:84%
could start limit pricing and
cut their prices on the Boston

00:55:45.940 --> 00:55:48.850 align:middle line:84%
to Nashville route to signal
to Southwest that they have

00:55:48.850 --> 00:55:51.910 align:middle line:84%
low cost and tell Southwest,
you should stay out

00:55:51.910 --> 00:55:55.510 align:middle line:90%
of Boston Nashville.

00:55:55.510 --> 00:55:58.270 align:middle line:84%
And the nice thing about
it is you can look at,

00:55:58.270 --> 00:56:02.740 align:middle line:84%
do they do this five months
before Southwest is even

00:56:02.740 --> 00:56:04.550 align:middle line:90%
operating Nashville to Miami?

00:56:04.550 --> 00:56:07.552 align:middle line:84%
So you've got this long period
before anything has actually

00:56:07.552 --> 00:56:09.010 align:middle line:84%
changed in the
airline market where

00:56:09.010 --> 00:56:11.450 align:middle line:84%
it's just the threat
of entry has changed.

00:56:11.450 --> 00:56:16.240 align:middle line:84%
And we look at with that
threat of entry changing,

00:56:16.240 --> 00:56:19.660 align:middle line:84%
does United start
changing its pricing,

00:56:19.660 --> 00:56:22.270 align:middle line:84%
and changing price in a way that
looks like it's limit pricing

00:56:22.270 --> 00:56:23.562 align:middle line:90%
or something to keep entry out?

00:56:23.562 --> 00:56:26.650 align:middle line:90%


00:56:26.650 --> 00:56:28.690 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, the interesting
results in the paper

00:56:28.690 --> 00:56:32.570 align:middle line:84%
are that this does
seem to go on.

00:56:32.570 --> 00:56:35.270 align:middle line:84%
So as they said,
Southwest typically

00:56:35.270 --> 00:56:39.830 align:middle line:84%
announces it's coming into an
end point airport like five

00:56:39.830 --> 00:56:41.270 align:middle line:90%
months before it does.

00:56:41.270 --> 00:56:42.800 align:middle line:90%
And if you look at--

00:56:42.800 --> 00:56:44.900 align:middle line:84%
so in this case,
Southwest has announced

00:56:44.900 --> 00:56:47.570 align:middle line:90%
that it's coming in at time T0.

00:56:47.570 --> 00:56:51.772 align:middle line:84%
And what you find is that
eight months before, you

00:56:51.772 --> 00:56:52.730 align:middle line:90%
don't pick up anything.

00:56:52.730 --> 00:56:56.540 align:middle line:84%
But then seven, six, five months
before, you pick up the airlines

00:56:56.540 --> 00:57:00.140 align:middle line:84%
having lowered
their prices by 7%.

00:57:00.140 --> 00:57:01.710 align:middle line:90%
It goes up to 10%.

00:57:01.710 --> 00:57:03.680 align:middle line:84%
And then by the
time Southwest is

00:57:03.680 --> 00:57:07.250 align:middle line:84%
almost in Nashville,
United's prices end up 13%

00:57:07.250 --> 00:57:09.990 align:middle line:84%
lower than they were
before the entry occurred.

00:57:09.990 --> 00:57:13.080 align:middle line:84%
And again, this is entry not
competing directly on the route.

00:57:13.080 --> 00:57:15.660 align:middle line:84%
This is just threat
of entry causes

00:57:15.660 --> 00:57:18.470 align:middle line:90%
this 13% decline in prices.

00:57:18.470 --> 00:57:22.880 align:middle line:84%
And prices are then even
lower after Southwest

00:57:22.880 --> 00:57:26.090 align:middle line:84%
gets to Nashville, and then
lower still if Southwest

00:57:26.090 --> 00:57:28.940 align:middle line:84%
actually comes in and starts
competing with United on Boston

00:57:28.940 --> 00:57:30.530 align:middle line:90%
to Nashville.

00:57:30.530 --> 00:57:36.240 align:middle line:84%
But what they're
finding is that there

00:57:36.240 --> 00:57:39.030 align:middle line:84%
are these price declines well
before anything has actually

00:57:39.030 --> 00:57:43.290 align:middle line:84%
changed in the markets
other than the future entry

00:57:43.290 --> 00:57:44.700 align:middle line:90%
possibility.

00:57:44.700 --> 00:57:47.520 align:middle line:84%
So I think it's a
striking finding.

00:57:47.520 --> 00:57:51.330 align:middle line:84%
It's not perfectly clean as a
reduced form prediction of they

00:57:51.330 --> 00:57:54.690 align:middle line:84%
must be behaving
strategically because this

00:57:54.690 --> 00:57:57.630 align:middle line:84%
is a model that would
have a competition effect.

00:57:57.630 --> 00:58:04.680 align:middle line:84%
If United knows that Southwest
is coming into the market,

00:58:04.680 --> 00:58:08.130 align:middle line:84%
then it knows that it's going
to be in a duopoly instead

00:58:08.130 --> 00:58:09.910 align:middle line:90%
of a monopoly in the future.

00:58:09.910 --> 00:58:11.580 align:middle line:84%
It, therefore,
might want to behave

00:58:11.580 --> 00:58:13.500 align:middle line:84%
in a way that's good
for it in a duopoly

00:58:13.500 --> 00:58:15.670 align:middle line:84%
relative to what's good
for it in a monopoly.

00:58:15.670 --> 00:58:20.047 align:middle line:84%
Now, my first thought would be,
actually, that one could even

00:58:20.047 --> 00:58:20.880 align:middle line:90%
go in the other way.

00:58:20.880 --> 00:58:22.980 align:middle line:84%
And it could be they want to
raise their prices because

00:58:22.980 --> 00:58:24.063 align:middle line:90%
of the competition effect.

00:58:24.063 --> 00:58:26.070 align:middle line:84%
Because if they've been
trying to build customer

00:58:26.070 --> 00:58:28.620 align:middle line:84%
loyalty over the years, and
build frequent flyer miles,

00:58:28.620 --> 00:58:32.305 align:middle line:84%
and get people to spend a lot
of money, it could be that,

00:58:32.305 --> 00:58:33.930 align:middle line:84%
again, you just start
eat your loyalty.

00:58:33.930 --> 00:58:35.870 align:middle line:84%
It's like, OK, people have
good feelings about us.

00:58:35.870 --> 00:58:37.840 align:middle line:84%
Good feelings are going
to be worthless to us.

00:58:37.840 --> 00:58:40.007 align:middle line:84%
Let's just go ahead and
make all the money we want--

00:58:40.007 --> 00:58:41.683 align:middle line:90%
$2,000 to fly to Nashville.

00:58:41.683 --> 00:58:42.850 align:middle line:90%
We'll take all our goodwill.

00:58:42.850 --> 00:58:44.392 align:middle line:84%
We'll burn it in
the next six months,

00:58:44.392 --> 00:58:45.810 align:middle line:90%
and then be done with it.

00:58:45.810 --> 00:58:50.340 align:middle line:84%
But it could also go the other
way if, by keeping prices low,

00:58:50.340 --> 00:58:51.970 align:middle line:84%
today people are
loyal to United,

00:58:51.970 --> 00:58:53.850 align:middle line:84%
and don't ever try
Southwest, and find out

00:58:53.850 --> 00:58:56.410 align:middle line:84%
what it's like to fly without
having a reserved seat.

00:58:56.410 --> 00:58:58.710 align:middle line:84%
And maybe you want to build
up the customer loyalty

00:58:58.710 --> 00:59:00.400 align:middle line:90%
and not get them to try--

00:59:00.400 --> 00:59:03.100 align:middle line:84%
get them so they don't feel like
they're not angry and wanting

00:59:03.100 --> 00:59:04.438 align:middle line:90%
to try Southwest.

00:59:04.438 --> 00:59:05.980 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, and then
a second problem

00:59:05.980 --> 00:59:10.220 align:middle line:84%
is that the pre-entry stuff,
I think, is really nice.

00:59:10.220 --> 00:59:13.960 align:middle line:84%
The post-entry estimates,
there's always this concern that

00:59:13.960 --> 00:59:19.300 align:middle line:84%
while Southwest is
not flying Boston to--

00:59:19.300 --> 00:59:21.520 align:middle line:84%
they eliminate markets
where Southwest is flying--

00:59:21.520 --> 00:59:24.300 align:middle line:90%


00:59:24.300 --> 00:59:27.440 align:middle line:84%
where Southwest announces
Nashville to Boston,

00:59:27.440 --> 00:59:30.550 align:middle line:84%
they also would eliminate
if Southwest announces

00:59:30.550 --> 00:59:33.520 align:middle line:84%
Nashville to Miami,
and then they already

00:59:33.520 --> 00:59:35.230 align:middle line:90%
fly Miami to Boston.

00:59:35.230 --> 00:59:37.540 align:middle line:84%
But they don't
eliminate it if you

00:59:37.540 --> 00:59:41.650 align:middle line:84%
can go from Nashville to
Chicago, and from Chicago

00:59:41.650 --> 00:59:43.660 align:middle line:84%
to Washington, and
then from Washington

00:59:43.660 --> 00:59:45.340 align:middle line:90%
to Boston on Southwest.

00:59:45.340 --> 00:59:48.220 align:middle line:84%
Those markets, they
don't eliminate.

00:59:48.220 --> 00:59:51.320 align:middle line:84%
People who fly Southwest
sometimes do things like that.

00:59:51.320 --> 00:59:54.620 align:middle line:84%
So it could be there is
actually some mismeasured real

00:59:54.620 --> 00:59:56.620 align:middle line:84%
competition there that
causes from people taking

00:59:56.620 --> 00:59:59.590 align:middle line:84%
these multi-leg Southwest
journeys to compete

00:59:59.590 --> 01:00:05.400 align:middle line:84%
with the Boston Nashville
flights that United has.

01:00:05.400 --> 01:00:07.140 align:middle line:90%
Any questions?

01:00:07.140 --> 01:00:09.780 align:middle line:84%
So the thing I wanted
to finish off today

01:00:09.780 --> 01:00:14.880 align:middle line:84%
is talk about, I think, a very
nice recent paper by Sweeting,

01:00:14.880 --> 01:00:17.820 align:middle line:84%
Roberts, and Gedge on dynamic
limit pricing in the airline

01:00:17.820 --> 01:00:18.700 align:middle line:90%
industry.

01:00:18.700 --> 01:00:22.935 align:middle line:84%
And this is very much,
again, I think I say over

01:00:22.935 --> 01:00:25.560 align:middle line:84%
and over again, you don't have
to be really original to succeed

01:00:25.560 --> 01:00:26.520 align:middle line:90%
in economics.

01:00:26.520 --> 01:00:30.220 align:middle line:84%
You can just take a good
paper and do it better.

01:00:30.220 --> 01:00:32.550 align:middle line:84%
And so what Sweeting, and
Roberts, and Gedge are doing

01:00:32.550 --> 01:00:38.570 align:middle line:84%
is looking at pricing by
incumbents when Southwest

01:00:38.570 --> 01:00:41.750 align:middle line:84%
is threatening entry,
and trying to understand,

01:00:41.750 --> 01:00:44.990 align:middle line:84%
does the limit pricing
story really hang together

01:00:44.990 --> 01:00:47.270 align:middle line:84%
to explain what we see in
the pricing in the months

01:00:47.270 --> 01:00:48.925 align:middle line:84%
before Southwest
enters a market?

01:00:48.925 --> 01:00:52.573 align:middle line:90%


01:00:52.573 --> 01:00:53.990 align:middle line:84%
So what they do
is they start out,

01:00:53.990 --> 01:00:57.230 align:middle line:84%
they discuss a theoretical model
in which the limit pricing would

01:00:57.230 --> 01:00:59.090 align:middle line:90%
make sense.

01:00:59.090 --> 01:01:01.580 align:middle line:84%
They do a test of
reduced form predictions

01:01:01.580 --> 01:01:04.760 align:middle line:84%
of that model versus
a non-strategic model,

01:01:04.760 --> 01:01:07.410 align:middle line:84%
and then they do a
structural estimation.

01:01:07.410 --> 01:01:09.470 align:middle line:84%
Are the magnitudes here
are sufficient to explain

01:01:09.470 --> 01:01:13.800 align:middle line:84%
those 13% price drops that
Goolsbee Syverson reported?

01:01:13.800 --> 01:01:16.100 align:middle line:90%
So anyway, what's their model?

01:01:16.100 --> 01:01:21.320 align:middle line:84%
So it's a multi-period
Milgrom-Roberts limit pricing

01:01:21.320 --> 01:01:23.310 align:middle line:84%
through signaling
your cost model.

01:01:23.310 --> 01:01:26.570 align:middle line:84%
So the incumbent has
some marginal cost

01:01:26.570 --> 01:01:31.130 align:middle line:84%
of carrying passengers, CIT, and
it follows some Markov process

01:01:31.130 --> 01:01:34.190 align:middle line:84%
on an interval, C
lower bar, C upper bar.

01:01:34.190 --> 01:01:37.190 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to assume it's
a highly persistent Markov

01:01:37.190 --> 01:01:40.710 align:middle line:84%
process, like an AR1 process
with a coefficient close to 1.

01:01:40.710 --> 01:01:45.120 align:middle line:84%
So costs are following an
AR1 process on this interval.

01:01:45.120 --> 01:01:48.770 align:middle line:84%
So at every point in
time, you're always

01:01:48.770 --> 01:01:51.950 align:middle line:84%
uncertain about what United's
cost is because it's always

01:01:51.950 --> 01:01:54.800 align:middle line:90%
different, and it's evolving.

01:01:54.800 --> 01:01:59.910 align:middle line:84%
Southwest has a fixed entry
cost KT, and it's drawn i.i.d.

01:01:59.910 --> 01:02:02.500 align:middle line:84%
from some density
in every period.

01:02:02.500 --> 01:02:05.160 align:middle line:84%
And so there can be periods
when it's good for Southwest

01:02:05.160 --> 01:02:07.120 align:middle line:84%
to enter and bad for
Southwest to enter.

01:02:07.120 --> 01:02:08.787 align:middle line:84%
They're going to be
more likely to enter

01:02:08.787 --> 01:02:11.280 align:middle line:90%
when their costs are low.

01:02:11.280 --> 01:02:16.740 align:middle line:84%
And so at each time, T, the
incumbent chooses some price.

01:02:16.740 --> 01:02:19.700 align:middle line:90%
It earns some profit.

01:02:19.700 --> 01:02:22.610 align:middle line:84%
Southwest observes a price
they charge, but doesn't

01:02:22.610 --> 01:02:24.910 align:middle line:90%
observe the profit or the cost.

01:02:24.910 --> 01:02:27.440 align:middle line:84%
And then Southwest
learns what its cost

01:02:27.440 --> 01:02:31.230 align:middle line:84%
draw is for entry costs, and
then it decides to enter or not.

01:02:31.230 --> 01:02:35.120 align:middle line:84%
And if it enters, there's
duopoly competition

01:02:35.120 --> 01:02:39.380 align:middle line:84%
with now commonly observed
costs in all future periods.

01:02:39.380 --> 01:02:41.670 align:middle line:84%
And firms just play the
static Nash equilibrium

01:02:41.670 --> 01:02:43.200 align:middle line:90%
of all future periods.

01:02:43.200 --> 01:02:45.560 align:middle line:84%
If Southwest doesn't
enter, then they just wait,

01:02:45.560 --> 01:02:47.060 align:middle line:84%
and they can enter
again next period

01:02:47.060 --> 01:02:48.540 align:middle line:90%
if they get a good cost draw.

01:02:48.540 --> 01:02:51.350 align:middle line:84%
So notice that Southwest, even
if a market is profitable,

01:02:51.350 --> 01:02:53.510 align:middle line:84%
may want to wait some
number of periods

01:02:53.510 --> 01:02:56.125 align:middle line:84%
to come in to wait
for a good cost draw.

01:02:56.125 --> 01:02:59.473 align:middle line:90%


01:02:59.473 --> 01:03:00.890 align:middle line:84%
And obviously,
they're going to be

01:03:00.890 --> 01:03:04.760 align:middle line:84%
more likely to come in if they
think that the incumbent's cost

01:03:04.760 --> 01:03:05.340 align:middle line:90%
is high.

01:03:05.340 --> 01:03:11.810 align:middle line:84%
This is a model where the
incumbent's cost is bouncing

01:03:11.810 --> 01:03:15.080 align:middle line:84%
around on some interval,
C lower bar C upper bar,

01:03:15.080 --> 01:03:18.680 align:middle line:84%
and Southwest just has some
other different costs, which I

01:03:18.680 --> 01:03:21.470 align:middle line:90%
think we think of as down here.

01:03:21.470 --> 01:03:23.780 align:middle line:84%
So we think of Southwest
as being a lower cost

01:03:23.780 --> 01:03:24.860 align:middle line:90%
carrier than United.

01:03:24.860 --> 01:03:27.965 align:middle line:84%
So it's such that if
United's cost is down here,

01:03:27.965 --> 01:03:29.840 align:middle line:84%
Southwest is going to
say, our cost advantage

01:03:29.840 --> 01:03:32.465 align:middle line:84%
isn't big enough to pay all the
fixed costs of buying the gates

01:03:32.465 --> 01:03:33.510 align:middle line:90%
and hiring the employees.

01:03:33.510 --> 01:03:35.892 align:middle line:84%
If United's cost is
up here, Southwest

01:03:35.892 --> 01:03:37.975 align:middle line:84%
is going to want to come
in and compete with them.

01:03:37.975 --> 01:03:41.224 align:middle line:90%


01:03:41.224 --> 01:03:44.320 align:middle line:84%
And this Markov process,
like Milgrom and Roberts

01:03:44.320 --> 01:03:47.830 align:middle line:84%
was this one time, you price
low to signal your low cost,

01:03:47.830 --> 01:03:50.270 align:middle line:84%
and then they learn your cost,
and then they enter or not.

01:03:50.270 --> 01:03:52.298 align:middle line:84%
Here, there's just this
perpetual incentive

01:03:52.298 --> 01:03:54.340 align:middle line:84%
to always signal your cost
because there's always

01:03:54.340 --> 01:03:56.660 align:middle line:90%
a new innovation to your cost.

01:03:56.660 --> 01:04:00.073 align:middle line:84%
And that creates this
incentive to continually limit

01:04:00.073 --> 01:04:01.990 align:middle line:84%
price, limit price, limit
price to always make

01:04:01.990 --> 01:04:03.240 align:middle line:90%
them think your cost is low.

01:04:03.240 --> 01:04:11.540 align:middle line:90%


01:04:11.540 --> 01:04:14.650 align:middle line:84%
So the first thing they
do in the paper is--

01:04:14.650 --> 01:04:17.980 align:middle line:84%
it's a non-monotonicity
test, and it's motivated

01:04:17.980 --> 01:04:20.410 align:middle line:90%
by the limit pricing model.

01:04:20.410 --> 01:04:23.590 align:middle line:84%
The model predicts
that limit pricing

01:04:23.590 --> 01:04:27.730 align:middle line:84%
is going to be most
important in markets

01:04:27.730 --> 01:04:30.050 align:middle line:84%
that have an intermediate
entry probability.

01:04:30.050 --> 01:04:33.883 align:middle line:84%
If you have a market where there
is a very low entry probability,

01:04:33.883 --> 01:04:35.800 align:middle line:84%
then you're not going
to bother limit pricing.

01:04:35.800 --> 01:04:37.758 align:middle line:84%
If there's a market where
there's a very high--

01:04:37.758 --> 01:04:44.730 align:middle line:84%
so the service from Boston
to Billings, Montana,

01:04:44.730 --> 01:04:47.570 align:middle line:84%
there's no way Southwest is
going to start flying Boston

01:04:47.570 --> 01:04:48.800 align:middle line:90%
to Billings, Montana.

01:04:48.800 --> 01:04:51.980 align:middle line:84%
It's just not something of
the kind of market they serve.

01:04:51.980 --> 01:04:55.280 align:middle line:84%
There, you don't
need to limit price.

01:04:55.280 --> 01:04:57.165 align:middle line:84%
If they've just
entered Las Vegas

01:04:57.165 --> 01:04:59.540 align:middle line:84%
and they're not serving it
yet, you know sooner or later,

01:04:59.540 --> 01:05:02.323 align:middle line:84%
there's going to be a Boston to
Las Vegas flight on Southwest.

01:05:02.323 --> 01:05:03.990 align:middle line:84%
There's nothing you
can do to stop that,

01:05:03.990 --> 01:05:05.700 align:middle line:84%
so you don't try to
limit price there.

01:05:05.700 --> 01:05:08.630 align:middle line:84%
But maybe Boston to Nashville
is an intermediate sized market

01:05:08.630 --> 01:05:12.230 align:middle line:84%
where you could try to
make that one unattractive.

01:05:12.230 --> 01:05:15.140 align:middle line:84%
And so what they're going
to say is, do incumbents

01:05:15.140 --> 01:05:18.020 align:middle line:84%
drop their prices
most in markets

01:05:18.020 --> 01:05:21.390 align:middle line:84%
with an intermediate
entry probability?

01:05:21.390 --> 01:05:26.310 align:middle line:84%
And so this is pretty simple,
again, econometric exercise.

01:05:26.310 --> 01:05:28.070 align:middle line:84%
They just have a
first stage regression

01:05:28.070 --> 01:05:31.400 align:middle line:84%
where they regress the
probability of Southwest entry

01:05:31.400 --> 01:05:38.180 align:middle line:84%
on market size, concentration
of whether the endpoints have

01:05:38.180 --> 01:05:42.210 align:middle line:84%
dominant carriers or not, how it
fits with Southwest's network,

01:05:42.210 --> 01:05:43.968 align:middle line:90%
other explanatory variables.

01:05:43.968 --> 01:05:45.510 align:middle line:84%
So they have a first
stage regression

01:05:45.510 --> 01:05:48.460 align:middle line:84%
trying to understand where
it is that Southwest enters.

01:05:48.460 --> 01:05:54.570 align:middle line:84%
And then the second stage, so
PM hat is the predicted entry

01:05:54.570 --> 01:05:55.920 align:middle line:90%
probability.

01:05:55.920 --> 01:05:57.820 align:middle line:84%
And so what they're
looking for is this--

01:05:57.820 --> 01:06:00.250 align:middle line:84%
I think this sounds for
Southwest post entry.

01:06:00.250 --> 01:06:06.360 align:middle line:84%
They're looking at is price
being reduced after entry

01:06:06.360 --> 01:06:08.782 align:middle line:84%
in markets where there's
an intermediate entry

01:06:08.782 --> 01:06:10.740 align:middle line:84%
probability which you
would get from the beta 1

01:06:10.740 --> 01:06:13.115 align:middle line:84%
being negative and the
beta 2 being positive?

01:06:13.115 --> 01:06:14.490 align:middle line:84%
It would be saying
that dips down

01:06:14.490 --> 01:06:17.610 align:middle line:84%
and has low prices in those
intermediate sized markets.

01:06:17.610 --> 01:06:20.643 align:middle line:84%
And so they just estimate
this entry probability

01:06:20.643 --> 01:06:22.560 align:middle line:84%
based on market
characteristics, and then they

01:06:22.560 --> 01:06:27.040 align:middle line:84%
look at how the prices
change related to that.

01:06:27.040 --> 01:06:34.210 align:middle line:84%
And pretty clean results
that those 13% price declines

01:06:34.210 --> 01:06:39.760 align:middle line:84%
are relatively larger in the
intermediate entry probability

01:06:39.760 --> 01:06:42.110 align:middle line:90%
markets.

01:06:42.110 --> 01:06:43.490 align:middle line:90%
So what you get is--

01:06:43.490 --> 01:06:46.340 align:middle line:84%
and they give many
different versions of this,

01:06:46.340 --> 01:06:49.190 align:middle line:84%
but the simplest one
is just log of price.

01:06:49.190 --> 01:06:53.180 align:middle line:84%
You regress-- This is the
change in log price after entry,

01:06:53.180 --> 01:06:57.590 align:middle line:84%
and you see that its interaction
with the probability of entry

01:06:57.590 --> 01:07:02.300 align:middle line:84%
is a negative coefficient
on the probability of entry,

01:07:02.300 --> 01:07:05.610 align:middle line:84%
a positive coefficient on the
probability of entry squared.

01:07:05.610 --> 01:07:07.430 align:middle line:84%
So it's as if that
effect is something

01:07:07.430 --> 01:07:09.790 align:middle line:84%
that goes down like that
and then goes up like that.

01:07:09.790 --> 01:07:12.630 align:middle line:90%


01:07:12.630 --> 01:07:14.640 align:middle line:84%
Another thing they do,
just on the quantity side

01:07:14.640 --> 01:07:16.182 align:middle line:84%
instead of on the
price side, is look

01:07:16.182 --> 01:07:17.980 align:middle line:90%
at how full are the airplanes.

01:07:17.980 --> 01:07:20.250 align:middle line:84%
Are they sending off
full or empty airplanes?

01:07:20.250 --> 01:07:22.350 align:middle line:84%
And again, this one,
as you would expect,

01:07:22.350 --> 01:07:24.210 align:middle line:90%
it goes the opposite way.

01:07:24.210 --> 01:07:27.480 align:middle line:90%
So this is the price.

01:07:27.480 --> 01:07:29.460 align:middle line:84%
If you look at how
full the airplanes

01:07:29.460 --> 01:07:32.370 align:middle line:84%
are, that one goes
up and then down so

01:07:32.370 --> 01:07:35.790 align:middle line:84%
that it's your more
filling up the planes

01:07:35.790 --> 01:07:37.227 align:middle line:84%
in the intermediate
sized markets,

01:07:37.227 --> 01:07:39.935 align:middle line:84%
which presumably you fill up the
planes by charging lower prices.

01:07:39.935 --> 01:07:42.490 align:middle line:90%


01:07:42.490 --> 01:07:44.590 align:middle line:84%
And all the others
seem to go same way.

01:07:44.590 --> 01:07:46.090 align:middle line:84%
But the story seems
to be that, yes,

01:07:46.090 --> 01:07:53.140 align:middle line:84%
it is the intermediate
sized markets where you're

01:07:53.140 --> 01:07:54.760 align:middle line:84%
unsure if they're
going to enter,

01:07:54.760 --> 01:07:58.820 align:middle line:84%
where the price
declines are biggest.

01:07:58.820 --> 01:08:00.920 align:middle line:84%
And then the final
thing they try to do

01:08:00.920 --> 01:08:03.960 align:middle line:90%
is a structural analysis.

01:08:03.960 --> 01:08:06.780 align:middle line:84%
So what they're trying to
do is take that Markov model

01:08:06.780 --> 01:08:11.030 align:middle line:84%
they wrote down,
calibrate that model,

01:08:11.030 --> 01:08:13.615 align:middle line:84%
and see whether that model
explains what it has.

01:08:13.615 --> 01:08:17.120 align:middle line:90%


01:08:17.120 --> 01:08:20.090 align:middle line:84%
So in particular, what they're
often trying to do here

01:08:20.090 --> 01:08:26.770 align:middle line:84%
is trying to not use the
data in the threat period,

01:08:26.770 --> 01:08:31.630 align:middle line:84%
calibrate the model using data
from either the pre-period

01:08:31.630 --> 01:08:36.910 align:middle line:84%
before Southwest announced,
or after the Southwest entry,

01:08:36.910 --> 01:08:40.130 align:middle line:84%
and then show that we
can calibrate this model.

01:08:40.130 --> 01:08:42.910 align:middle line:84%
And that model actually does
predict price declines close

01:08:42.910 --> 01:08:45.170 align:middle line:90%
to the actual price declines.

01:08:45.170 --> 01:08:49.062 align:middle line:84%
So it's an exercise of saying,
we're going to take this model.

01:08:49.062 --> 01:08:51.520 align:middle line:84%
We're going to calibrate it
using the data that we have not

01:08:51.520 --> 01:08:53.109 align:middle line:84%
from that strategic
investment period,

01:08:53.109 --> 01:08:54.550 align:middle line:84%
and then say, what
does it predict

01:08:54.550 --> 01:08:56.899 align:middle line:84%
should happen in the
strategic investment period?

01:08:56.899 --> 01:09:00.040 align:middle line:84%
And yes, it does seem to
predict the data pretty well.

01:09:00.040 --> 01:09:01.600 align:middle line:84%
And then once they
have it, they also

01:09:01.600 --> 01:09:04.029 align:middle line:84%
use it to discuss
some welfare questions

01:09:04.029 --> 01:09:07.090 align:middle line:84%
because it's going to be a
model that you get back consumer

01:09:07.090 --> 01:09:08.819 align:middle line:90%
utility and prices out of it.

01:09:08.819 --> 01:09:11.319 align:middle line:84%
You can estimate deadweight
losses, and see how they change,

01:09:11.319 --> 01:09:13.694 align:middle line:84%
and talk about the welfare
effects of this limit pricing.

01:09:13.694 --> 01:09:15.927 align:middle line:90%


01:09:15.927 --> 01:09:17.130 align:middle line:90%
So how is this done?

01:09:17.130 --> 01:09:22.500 align:middle line:84%
So they restrict
themselves to 109 markets.

01:09:22.500 --> 01:09:25.160 align:middle line:84%
So these are 109
city pair markets,

01:09:25.160 --> 01:09:27.229 align:middle line:84%
and they're all doing--
to get as close as

01:09:27.229 --> 01:09:28.854 align:middle line:84%
possible to this
monopoly case, they're

01:09:28.854 --> 01:09:31.040 align:middle line:84%
doing 109 markets where
there's one carrier that

01:09:31.040 --> 01:09:36.170 align:middle line:84%
has a dominant share
before Southwest comes in.

01:09:36.170 --> 01:09:38.880 align:middle line:84%
Many markets, there are
multiple carriers serving them.

01:09:38.880 --> 01:09:41.990 align:middle line:84%
But if you pick smaller city
pair markets, a lot of them

01:09:41.990 --> 01:09:44.262 align:middle line:84%
that have one carrier
doing most of the traffic.

01:09:44.262 --> 01:09:46.220 align:middle line:84%
So you can think of it
as there's a monopolist,

01:09:46.220 --> 01:09:50.170 align:middle line:84%
and then the monopolist might
be competing with Southwest.

01:09:50.170 --> 01:09:54.400 align:middle line:84%
What they assume is that demand
is a nested logit model where

01:09:54.400 --> 01:09:57.400 align:middle line:84%
you have the outside good
of not flying in one nest,

01:09:57.400 --> 01:10:02.260 align:middle line:84%
and then Southwest versus
United in the inside nest.

01:10:02.260 --> 01:10:07.120 align:middle line:84%
And then they estimate
that model using data

01:10:07.120 --> 01:10:14.890 align:middle line:84%
from before there's any
threat, and using data

01:10:14.890 --> 01:10:16.265 align:middle line:84%
from after Southwest
has entered.

01:10:16.265 --> 01:10:18.182 align:middle line:84%
And then you're just
observing the competition

01:10:18.182 --> 01:10:20.030 align:middle line:84%
between the other
airline and Southwest.

01:10:20.030 --> 01:10:24.280 align:middle line:84%
And where do they get
the IVs to do this?

01:10:24.280 --> 01:10:27.460 align:middle line:84%
In the pre-period,
It's fairly easy.

01:10:27.460 --> 01:10:31.120 align:middle line:84%
In the pre-period, they're
getting the IV for your price

01:10:31.120 --> 01:10:33.200 align:middle line:90%
from fuel prices.

01:10:33.200 --> 01:10:35.320 align:middle line:84%
When fuel prices go up,
airlines raise prices.

01:10:35.320 --> 01:10:38.120 align:middle line:84%
When fuel prices go down,
airlines lower prices.

01:10:38.120 --> 01:10:41.020 align:middle line:84%
Looking at how demand
changes with fuel prices,

01:10:41.020 --> 01:10:43.240 align:middle line:84%
they estimate substitution
between the inside good

01:10:43.240 --> 01:10:44.830 align:middle line:90%
and the outside good.

01:10:44.830 --> 01:10:48.310 align:middle line:84%
And then for the
price competition

01:10:48.310 --> 01:10:53.420 align:middle line:84%
between Southwest and United,
they're not as great endpoints,

01:10:53.420 --> 01:10:56.593 align:middle line:84%
but it's like, using
United's endpoint shares,

01:10:56.593 --> 01:10:58.760 align:middle line:84%
and where United has bigger
shares at the endpoints,

01:10:58.760 --> 01:11:00.390 align:middle line:84%
they're going to tend
to set higher prices.

01:11:00.390 --> 01:11:02.840 align:middle line:84%
That's going to change the
relative prices of Southwest

01:11:02.840 --> 01:11:05.390 align:middle line:90%
and United.

01:11:05.390 --> 01:11:08.570 align:middle line:84%
Then the marginal
cost, going back

01:11:08.570 --> 01:11:15.350 align:middle line:84%
to what Tobias lectures, if
you first estimate the demand

01:11:15.350 --> 01:11:18.315 align:middle line:84%
elasticities, and then
you assume that firms

01:11:18.315 --> 01:11:19.940 align:middle line:84%
are pricing optimally,
you can back out

01:11:19.940 --> 01:11:21.543 align:middle line:90%
what the marginal costs are.

01:11:21.543 --> 01:11:23.960 align:middle line:84%
So they do that kind of approach
where they first estimate

01:11:23.960 --> 01:11:25.230 align:middle line:90%
the demand elasticities.

01:11:25.230 --> 01:11:27.230 align:middle line:84%
Then given the
demand elasticities,

01:11:27.230 --> 01:11:29.960 align:middle line:84%
assume price firms are
pricing at the static optimum.

01:11:29.960 --> 01:11:33.190 align:middle line:84%
They back out what
the costs are.

01:11:33.190 --> 01:11:37.010 align:middle line:84%
And then once they get
a cost period by period,

01:11:37.010 --> 01:11:38.980 align:middle line:84%
they just run
regressions on them

01:11:38.980 --> 01:11:40.870 align:middle line:84%
to estimate AR1
coefficients and find

01:11:40.870 --> 01:11:43.270 align:middle line:90%
if they're serially correlated.

01:11:43.270 --> 01:11:48.370 align:middle line:84%
And then for entry
costs, they just

01:11:48.370 --> 01:11:51.430 align:middle line:84%
take a subset of the data
using 20% of the markets,

01:11:51.430 --> 01:11:54.460 align:middle line:84%
and they just say what
distribution of entry costs--

01:11:54.460 --> 01:11:57.460 align:middle line:84%
given the demand estimates that
we have and the profit function

01:11:57.460 --> 01:12:00.340 align:middle line:84%
we've estimated for Southwest,
what entry costs would

01:12:00.340 --> 01:12:02.140 align:middle line:84%
rationalize the
observed level of entry

01:12:02.140 --> 01:12:04.900 align:middle line:84%
and the observed correlation
between entry and market size?

01:12:04.900 --> 01:12:07.420 align:middle line:84%
And they estimate the
entry cost distributions

01:12:07.420 --> 01:12:08.950 align:middle line:90%
to match that to the data.

01:12:08.950 --> 01:12:11.830 align:middle line:84%
So that's where the
primitives come from.

01:12:11.830 --> 01:12:13.450 align:middle line:90%
What do they look like?

01:12:13.450 --> 01:12:15.700 align:middle line:84%
OK, so here's what
they look like.

01:12:15.700 --> 01:12:17.770 align:middle line:84%
The demand estimates
find that these goods

01:12:17.770 --> 01:12:19.840 align:middle line:90%
are close substitutes.

01:12:19.840 --> 01:12:25.300 align:middle line:84%
After entry, the average own
price elasticity for incumbents

01:12:25.300 --> 01:12:27.730 align:middle line:90%
is minus 2.9.

01:12:27.730 --> 01:12:30.980 align:middle line:84%
So you're still going to get
30% markups over marginal cost,

01:12:30.980 --> 01:12:34.120 align:middle line:84%
but demand is fairly
elastic after people

01:12:34.120 --> 01:12:37.300 align:middle line:90%
start competing with Southwest.

01:12:37.300 --> 01:12:39.670 align:middle line:84%
The marginal cost
estimates suggest

01:12:39.670 --> 01:12:43.270 align:middle line:84%
that the traditional carriers
are much more expensive, much

01:12:43.270 --> 01:12:46.300 align:middle line:84%
higher costs of operation
than Southwest does.

01:12:46.300 --> 01:12:50.140 align:middle line:84%
The marginal costs
are averaging 258--

01:12:50.140 --> 01:12:53.470 align:middle line:84%
the backed out marginal cost
average 258 for the incumbents,

01:12:53.470 --> 01:12:56.330 align:middle line:90%
and 168 for Southwest.

01:12:56.330 --> 01:12:59.274 align:middle line:84%
So it looks like Southwest has
a big cost advantage, which

01:12:59.274 --> 01:13:00.857 align:middle line:84%
reflected comes out
in the data of you

01:13:00.857 --> 01:13:03.280 align:middle line:84%
see Southwest just
pricing much lower

01:13:03.280 --> 01:13:06.160 align:middle line:90%
than their traditional rivals.

01:13:06.160 --> 01:13:12.010 align:middle line:84%
And when you do the serial
correlation of the costs,

01:13:12.010 --> 01:13:14.800 align:middle line:84%
it looks like costs are
very highly serially

01:13:14.800 --> 01:13:15.945 align:middle line:90%
correlated over time.

01:13:15.945 --> 01:13:19.490 align:middle line:90%


01:13:19.490 --> 01:13:21.440 align:middle line:84%
And now on the
calibrated entry costs,

01:13:21.440 --> 01:13:25.586 align:middle line:84%
they just say that
we can match the--

01:13:25.586 --> 01:13:28.970 align:middle line:84%
this graph here is putting
two things in the paper.

01:13:28.970 --> 01:13:32.720 align:middle line:84%
On the x-axis
they've gotten the--

01:13:32.720 --> 01:13:36.890 align:middle line:84%
x-axis is the predicted
entry probability

01:13:36.890 --> 01:13:41.180 align:middle line:84%
from a regression of entry
on market characteristics.

01:13:41.180 --> 01:13:43.670 align:middle line:84%
The y-axis is the
predicted entry cost

01:13:43.670 --> 01:13:46.730 align:middle line:84%
that comes out of the
theoretical model with the entry

01:13:46.730 --> 01:13:49.220 align:middle line:84%
cost they've calibrated to
make the two curves as close

01:13:49.220 --> 01:13:50.670 align:middle line:90%
to each other as possible.

01:13:50.670 --> 01:13:53.570 align:middle line:84%
And they notice that they can
make these two curves look

01:13:53.570 --> 01:13:56.660 align:middle line:84%
very, very close by picking
an appropriate entry cost

01:13:56.660 --> 01:13:58.850 align:middle line:90%
distribution.

01:13:58.850 --> 01:14:01.850 align:middle line:84%
So it's both measuring
the-- you have

01:14:01.850 --> 01:14:04.490 align:middle line:84%
to match both the level of
entry and the correlation

01:14:04.490 --> 01:14:06.710 align:middle line:84%
between entry and market
size, but it looks

01:14:06.710 --> 01:14:07.990 align:middle line:90%
like they're able to do that.

01:14:07.990 --> 01:14:14.220 align:middle line:90%


01:14:14.220 --> 01:14:19.240 align:middle line:84%
So two things they then do
with the calibrated model.

01:14:19.240 --> 01:14:22.290 align:middle line:90%
One is, does this model--

01:14:22.290 --> 01:14:27.185 align:middle line:84%
is the limit pricing
story explaining

01:14:27.185 --> 01:14:30.120 align:middle line:84%
how much limit pricing
is going on in the data?

01:14:30.120 --> 01:14:33.690 align:middle line:90%
So panel B here is the data.

01:14:33.690 --> 01:14:36.570 align:middle line:90%


01:14:36.570 --> 01:14:39.090 align:middle line:84%
How much are incumbents
reducing price

01:14:39.090 --> 01:14:42.450 align:middle line:84%
as a function of the probability
that entry will occur

01:14:42.450 --> 01:14:44.580 align:middle line:90%
within the first four periods?

01:14:44.580 --> 01:14:47.460 align:middle line:84%
And here, I guess
you can see the data

01:14:47.460 --> 01:14:52.620 align:middle line:84%
in theirs must apparently
be not quite as large

01:14:52.620 --> 01:14:54.030 align:middle line:90%
a price reduction as--

01:14:54.030 --> 01:14:56.400 align:middle line:84%
maybe it's fine if
these are things.

01:14:56.400 --> 01:15:01.980 align:middle line:84%
It's like, we're getting in
these intermediate size markets.

01:15:01.980 --> 01:15:05.700 align:middle line:84%
We're getting 13% or
14% price reductions.

01:15:05.700 --> 01:15:08.380 align:middle line:84%
In these markets with very
low entry probabilities,

01:15:08.380 --> 01:15:11.050 align:middle line:84%
we're getting like
4% price reductions.

01:15:11.050 --> 01:15:12.840 align:middle line:90%
And then in very big--

01:15:12.840 --> 01:15:15.580 align:middle line:84%
in markets where there's almost
sure entry, although there

01:15:15.580 --> 01:15:19.930 align:middle line:84%
are very few such markets,
they're getting price increases.

01:15:19.930 --> 01:15:23.000 align:middle line:84%
So that's what the
data look like.

01:15:23.000 --> 01:15:26.820 align:middle line:84%
And then this is what
the model predicts.

01:15:26.820 --> 01:15:29.970 align:middle line:84%
The model predicts that
in very small markets

01:15:29.970 --> 01:15:33.600 align:middle line:84%
you would have very, very tiny
price cuts-- just a couple

01:15:33.600 --> 01:15:34.590 align:middle line:90%
percent.

01:15:34.590 --> 01:15:36.330 align:middle line:84%
In the intermediate
sized markets,

01:15:36.330 --> 01:15:39.190 align:middle line:90%
you would have 17% price cuts.

01:15:39.190 --> 01:15:40.860 align:middle line:84%
And then as we go
to this extreme,

01:15:40.860 --> 01:15:43.230 align:middle line:90%
we're getting 9% price cuts.

01:15:43.230 --> 01:15:48.620 align:middle line:84%
So again, it's not like the
model is exactly fitting

01:15:48.620 --> 01:15:50.330 align:middle line:90%
the data, but the model is--

01:15:50.330 --> 01:15:54.080 align:middle line:84%
it's the model that they've
estimated not using the pricing

01:15:54.080 --> 01:15:56.522 align:middle line:84%
data from the period when
the threat of entry occurred.

01:15:56.522 --> 01:15:57.980 align:middle line:84%
And this is what
the model predicts

01:15:57.980 --> 01:15:59.848 align:middle line:84%
will happen in
that threat period.

01:15:59.848 --> 01:16:01.890 align:middle line:84%
And this is what did happen
in the threat period,

01:16:01.890 --> 01:16:04.958 align:middle line:84%
and they argue that it seems
like quantitatively, the model

01:16:04.958 --> 01:16:07.250 align:middle line:84%
is doing a pretty good job
of explaining how much limit

01:16:07.250 --> 01:16:09.390 align:middle line:90%
pricing there is.

01:16:09.390 --> 01:16:13.530 align:middle line:84%
And so we'll say, they think the
limit pricing is explaining why

01:16:13.530 --> 01:16:15.420 align:middle line:84%
this is-- the signaling
is explaining--

01:16:15.420 --> 01:16:17.362 align:middle line:90%
could be explaining--

01:16:17.362 --> 01:16:19.320 align:middle line:84%
let me say, signaling is
of the right magnitude

01:16:19.320 --> 01:16:23.271 align:middle line:84%
to explain why
this is happening.

01:16:23.271 --> 01:16:24.660 align:middle line:84%
And then the second
thing they do

01:16:24.660 --> 01:16:27.580 align:middle line:84%
is talk about what are
the welfare effects.

01:16:27.580 --> 01:16:32.905 align:middle line:84%
And I guess, not
surprisingly, limit pricing

01:16:32.905 --> 01:16:34.530 align:middle line:84%
is really good here
compared to a model

01:16:34.530 --> 01:16:35.760 align:middle line:90%
with known incumbent costs.

01:16:35.760 --> 01:16:39.860 align:middle line:84%
Because those limit pricing
models are like this--

01:16:39.860 --> 01:16:43.290 align:middle line:90%


01:16:43.290 --> 01:16:46.590 align:middle line:84%
if you think about relative
to complete information,

01:16:46.590 --> 01:16:48.660 align:middle line:84%
firms are pricing a lot
lower, and they're not

01:16:48.660 --> 01:16:51.010 align:middle line:90%
affecting the entry much.

01:16:51.010 --> 01:17:01.050 align:middle line:84%
And so you know
by pricing lower,

01:17:01.050 --> 01:17:03.810 align:middle line:84%
there's a substantial
increase in consumer surplus

01:17:03.810 --> 01:17:05.440 align:middle line:90%
from the limit pricing.

01:17:05.440 --> 01:17:08.680 align:middle line:84%
There's a loss in incumbent
profits from the limit pricing,

01:17:08.680 --> 01:17:11.290 align:middle line:84%
but essentially, we're
eliminating the deadweight loss.

01:17:11.290 --> 01:17:13.020 align:middle line:84%
And so by limiting
the deadweight loss,

01:17:13.020 --> 01:17:16.010 align:middle line:90%
we're making things much better.

01:17:16.010 --> 01:17:18.510 align:middle line:84%
And the only place where they
argue that this doesn't happen

01:17:18.510 --> 01:17:22.390 align:middle line:84%
is in the very large
markets where there's

01:17:22.390 --> 01:17:23.980 align:middle line:90%
much limit pricing going on.

01:17:23.980 --> 01:17:27.910 align:middle line:84%
There, there's not really
much of a welfare gain

01:17:27.910 --> 01:17:30.890 align:middle line:84%
because the incumbents don't
really do much limit pricing.

01:17:30.890 --> 01:17:33.560 align:middle line:84%
But in general, the limit
pricing just lowers prices.

01:17:33.560 --> 01:17:35.320 align:middle line:84%
You have these consumer
preferences where

01:17:35.320 --> 01:17:36.950 align:middle line:90%
that reduces deadweight loss.

01:17:36.950 --> 01:17:39.795 align:middle line:84%
This limit pricing is a
socially beneficial thing.

01:17:39.795 --> 01:17:46.300 align:middle line:90%


01:17:46.300 --> 01:17:48.130 align:middle line:90%
OK, questions?

01:17:48.130 --> 01:17:49.170 align:middle line:90%
No?

01:17:49.170 --> 01:17:49.670 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:17:49.670 --> 01:17:55.680 align:middle line:84%
So I just want to say,
very nice structural paper

01:17:55.680 --> 01:17:59.720 align:middle line:90%
on entry deterrence.

01:17:59.720 --> 01:18:02.160 align:middle line:84%
Stephen Ryan has
a very nice paper.

01:18:02.160 --> 01:18:04.920 align:middle line:84%
I'm leaving it for [14.]273
because it uses a lot

01:18:04.920 --> 01:18:07.900 align:middle line:84%
of methodology that's
covered in [14.]273.

01:18:07.900 --> 01:18:10.470 align:middle line:84%
But the basic thing
of Stephen's paper

01:18:10.470 --> 01:18:18.458 align:middle line:84%
is looking at when you raise
environmental regulations, one

01:18:18.458 --> 01:18:20.250 align:middle line:84%
of the costs the
environmental regulations,

01:18:20.250 --> 01:18:21.780 align:middle line:84%
is they make entry
more difficult.

01:18:21.780 --> 01:18:25.440 align:middle line:84%
But then the incumbents don't
have to strategically deter

01:18:25.440 --> 01:18:28.620 align:middle line:84%
entry anymore, so you can
get much higher pricing

01:18:28.620 --> 01:18:33.330 align:middle line:84%
as an additional cost of
environmental regulation.

01:18:33.330 --> 01:18:36.430 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, that's covered
well in [14.]273.

01:18:36.430 --> 01:18:39.990 align:middle line:84%
So next week, again,
might shift gears.

01:18:39.990 --> 01:18:43.060 align:middle line:84%
Next week, I'm going to talk
about bounded rationality.

01:18:43.060 --> 01:18:45.960 align:middle line:84%
Not 100% sure what I'm going
do with those two lectures.

01:18:45.960 --> 01:18:47.860 align:middle line:90%
I may mix them up somewhat.

01:18:47.860 --> 01:18:50.220 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, next week, I'll
be doing something talking

01:18:50.220 --> 01:18:52.880 align:middle line:90%
about bounded rationality.

01:18:52.880 --> 01:19:06.000 align:middle line:90%